"580.0674\"","\"386.7116\"","\"axis\"","\"value\"","\"result\"","\"whole_share_cap\"","\"debt_cap_x_rate_without_liquid_assets\"","\"hard_issue_bound\"","\"growth_rld_x_margin_rld\"","\"ar_days_x_terminal_k_rld\"","\"case\"","\"inputs\"","\"GOV3\"","\"5.18\"","\"1.00\"","\"6.50\"","\"6.00\"","\"Avoid — governance\"","\"GOV4\"","\"5.38\"","\"5.4\"","\"6.5950\"","\"6.6\"","\"G1_FAIL\"","\"FAIL\"","\"5.58\"","\"G3_FAIL\"","\"5.6\"","\"6.6450\"","\"Speculative / distressed — funding\"","\"ALL_NUMERIC\"","\"NS_GOV3\"","\"4.34\"","\"4.46\"","\"5.54\"","\"2.1\"","\"CASH4_NO_CAP\"","\"5.68\"","\"5.7\"","\"6.60\"","\"6.6700\"","\"6.7\"","\"PERSISTENT_CAP3\"","\"5.40\"","\"6.20\"","\"6.6000\"","\"TRANSIENT_CAP3\"","\"6.2\"","\"Research artifact; independent review recorded separately.\"","\"# Remus Pharmaceuticals Limited\\n## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\\n**HOLD–UNRESOLVED. Not ranked; not an underwritten compounder.** Remus combines an outsourced-formulation export and registration business with a much larger, thin-margin US reference-listed-drug (RLD) distribution business acquired from promoter-related entities. The strength is genuine growth in the original business and access to international distribution; the weakness is the conversion of consolidated growth into cash available to the listed-company shareholder. At ₹984.50, market capitalisation is ₹1160.13 crore and corrected owner-earnings P/E is 31.36x, not the 25.13x obtained from total group PAT. A substantial Senores shareholding is valuable but is not operating cash or proof of a cheap stock. The unresolved EPS/minority reconciliations, poor group cash conversion and sensitivity to intra-period funding prevent a dependable entry valuation. The thesis is a **growth-plus-investment-holding special situation**, not a proven asset-light compounder. A cleaner legal-entity cash bridge and earnings reconciliation, followed by an adequately funded return above the hurdle, would change this conclusion. [AR26, AR25, RESIGN24, MASTER, TAPE]\\n\\n## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\\n- Exact issuer: **Remus Pharmaceuticals Limited**, CIN L24232GJ2015PLC084536, **ISIN INE0O5T01011**, NSE symbol REMUS. Do not confuse it with unrelated “Remus” pharmaceutical brands.\\n- Information cutoff: **20 October 2026, India time**. Market reference: **19 October 2026 official NSE closing price ₹984.50**. Source retrieval occurred later, but no later earnings, corporate action or market price enters the valuation.\\n- The active security is **SM / NSE EMERGE**. The September 2026 migration proposal received shareholder approval reported on 7 October, but that is not a completed main-board migration. Keep this research on the SME page. The exchange master supplies **11,784,000 issued shares** and **100-share lot**. Capitalisation is calculated with actual shares, not an unadjusted pre-bonus denominator.\\n- Scope: consolidated FY24–FY26 for the investable group; separately retained standalone FY20–FY26 for original-business history. FY24 is the later Ind AS comparative, not the unreconciled original Indian-GAAP vintage. Group five-year CAGR is **not available**.\\n- Horizon: five years from the reference entry, preceded by a projected 202-day audited-balance-sheet-to-entry bridge. Required return reference **15%**, with **12% and 18%** hurdles. ₹5 lakh liquidity reference is illustrative, not a stated user holding.\\n- **High confidence:** audited line-item transcriptions, exact security identity, corporate ownership chain and official daily trading observations. **Medium:** organic growth, underlying profitability and historical capital returns because of segment/perimeter changes and presentation issues. **Low:** current cash availability by legal entity, sustainable segment cash margins, current product-level approval register, financing commitments, and terminal realisability of investment assets.\\n\\n|Gate|Status|Reason and consequence|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|G1 Integrity / governance|Conditional, not Fail|No substantiated fraud or default finding; a real historical audit qualification, related-party acquisition and material RPT warrant scrutiny. Do not turn absence of an adverse finding into a clean bill of health.|\\n|G2 Accounting / evidence|Hold–unresolved|Reported EPS includes NCI earnings; subsidiary allocation tables do not reconcile cleanly; lease cash-flow classification and balance confirmations need clarification. Owner EPS can be corrected arithmetically, but segment economics cannot be repaired by guesswork.|\\n|G3 Funding / solvency|Conditional|Audited net assets are ample. However, demand borrowings, cash location and timing of collections matter. Optional refinancing, share issuance and investment sales are not committed liquidity. Model breaches describe assumptions, not an assertion of an actual company default.|\\n|G4 Investability / liquidity|Conditional|Active SME trading and lot size verified; spread, executable order book, current surveillance and binding circuit details not verified.|\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\\nThe actual v3.0 framework, updated 3 October 2026, is applied without substituting an earlier framework. Its SHA-256 is 68d4de4255427df276318d0231530b867a453e740c74150e9768bf1387224137. All arithmetic uses Decimal precision 50. Display rounding is half-up; no intermediate rounding or missing-factor renormalisation.\\n\\n|Factor|Score|Confidence|Evidence IDs and limitation|\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n|GOV|5|Medium|AR23, AR24, AR26, RESIGN24, PLEDGE26: Current unmodified audits and controls, but historical qualifications, cash/earnings presentation, related parties and minority reconciliation reduce confidence.|\\n|MOAT|5|Medium|AR26, CALL26, FDA, OK-LICENSE: Country registrations, distributor relationships and RLD sourcing capability; no patent or owned FDA-plant moat, and the current jurisdictional licence register is incomplete.|\\n|REV|8|Medium|IPO, AR23, AR25, AR26, FY26: Standalone revenue grew from ₹11.919 crore in FY20 to ₹93.99 crore in FY26, with recent existing-scope group growth. This is not a spliced acquired-group five-year CAGR; acquisition and denominator limits prevent 9/10.|\\n|DUR|6|Medium|CALL25, CALL26, DECK26: Generic penetration, recurring comparator-drug needs and dossiers provide runway, conditional on funding, currency, launch and regulatory execution.|\\n|CAP|NS|Low|AR24, AR25, AR26: No reliable cohort or legal-entity incremental operating-capital bridge after acquisition and valuation changes. The reconciled broad proxy is reported but insufficient for attribution.|\\n|CASH|3|High|AR25, AR26: FY24–FY26 aggregate group CFO ₹26.49 crore / PAT ₹108.87 crore = 24.33%. Large receivables and inventory cause persistent weak conversion despite parent improvement.|\\n|BAL|6|Medium|AR26, TAPE: Large financial assets versus modest borrowings, but investments are not free cash; demand debt, FX, entity transfer and historical guarantee risks remain.|\\n|EXEC|5|Medium|CALL25, CALL26, AR26: Revenue execution is strong. B2C 14–15% trails the May 2025 management ambition of 25–30% and the 18–20% target recalled by May 2026 questioners. Registration cohorts are unverified and the clinical unit is pre-revenue.|\\n|EARN|5|Medium|AR25, AR26: Owner earnings grow, but forfeitures and OCI need stripping. EPS uses total PAT including NCI and subsidiary allocations conflict.|\\n|R|NS|Low|AR26, model assumptions: Cash forecasts are not current verified balances; legal-entity minority and free-cash claims remain unresolved. Conditional scenario returns are not underwritable expected returns.|\\n|D|NS|Low|AR26, model assumptions: Cash-breach scenarios cannot have ordinary drawdown valuations. Realisability and entity claims remain unresolved.|\\n|P|NS|Low|AR26, MASTER, TAPE, model assumptions: Observed owner multiples are corrected, but cash-funded intrinsic value and ownership/asset realisation are not sufficiently reliable for a price-versus-value score.|\\n|L|7|Medium|MASTER, TAPE: Framework starting score 9 at 1.971 theoretical days, explicitly reduced to 7 for 100-share lots, top-five-session concentration of 41.85% and the lower 20-session median. Median 5% capacity is two whole lots; unknown spreads/circuits are not adverse facts.|\\n\\n|Composite|Known weighted sum|Coverage|Exact lower–upper before cap|Final|Postcap display bounds|Confidence|\\n|---|---:|---:|---|---|---|---|\\n|BQS|4.74|88.00%|4.86–5.94|NS|4.9–5.9|Low, missing factors|\\n|GQS|5.45|85.00%|5.60–6.95|NS|5.0–5.0|Low, missing factors|\\n|IAS|0.70|10.00%|2.5650–8.6850|NS|2.6–8.7|Low, missing factors|\\n\\n**Exact contribution audit:** BQS = GOV 5×.20 + MOAT 5×.20 + REV 8×.12 + DUR 6×.10 + CAP NS×.12 + CASH 3×.10 + BAL 6×.08 + EXEC 5×.08. Known subtotal 4.74, coverage 88%, raw interval **4.86–5.94**, final **NS**. GQS = REV 8×.40 + DUR 6×.20 + EARN 5×.15 + CAP NS×.15 + CASH 3×.10. Known subtotal 5.45, coverage 85%, raw interval **5.60–6.95**. Persistent poor cash conversion with CASH=3 caps the interval at **5–5**, but **does not make the missing composite a numeric 5**. Final remains NS. IAS uses final BQS×.25, R×.35, D×.20, P×.10 and L×.10. Only L is available at the outer level, so coverage is 10%, known subtotal .70; nested BQS uncertainty gives exact interval **2.565–8.685**, final NS. Interval endpoints are bounds, not estimates or rankings.\\n\\nG1 is not Fail and GOV is not ≤3, so the integrity cap of 3 is not activated. G3 is not categorically Fail, so its IAS cap is not activated. No discretionary override fills CAP/R/D/P. CAP is missing because a broad capital bridge is not enough to identify incremental operating returns by acquired business. R/D/P remain missing because conditional scenario prices, especially after a funding breach, are not underwritable expected returns, downside protection or valuation support. Confidence in a displayed score is distinct from its numerical value. [AR23–AR26, CALL25–26; sections 8–10 below]\\n\\n## 4. Business Overview\\n\\n**Two different businesses, plus a financial investment.** The parent develops dossiers, coordinates third-party manufacturing, registers and markets finished formulations in overseas markets. It does not earn a manufacturing moat merely by using approved contract manufacturers. Management described 35 approved manufacturers, about 20 active, with one related-group supplier and the remainder external, in the May 2026 call. Supplier substitution still requires quality, dossier and country-specific continuity. Product development, bioequivalence work, registration, pharmacovigilance, inventory, local distribution and collections remain economic obligations even without owning a large plant. [CALL26]\\n\\nThe US business sources comparator medicines used in research and bioequivalence work. This is pharmaceutical procurement and distribution, not ownership of innovator patents or a stream of patent-protected prescription royalties. FY26 RLD sales of **₹740.46 crore** represent approximately **86.7% of ₹853.63 crore group revenue**. Other disclosed revenue comprises formulations ₹93.48 crore, APIs ₹3.55 crore, retention/services ₹1.10 crore, export incentives ₹.81 crore and **advance forfeitures ₹14.23 crore**. Forfeitures must not be projected as repeat product demand. The model starts from recurring revenue of ₹839.40 crore, split into RLD ₹740.46 crore and other ₹98.94 crore. [AR26 note 30]\\n\\n**Who owns the economics:** Remus owns 56.67% of Espee Global Holdings LLC; the holding entity owns 90% of Espee Biopharma & Finechem LLC, giving **51.003% effective ownership**, not 100% and not a direct 51% shareholding. Relius entities in Bolivia and Guatemala are 99%-owned; the new Espee Clinical Trials business is 68%-owned. The clinical unit was incorporated in July 2025 and had no revenue and a small loss in FY26. The group statements consolidate 100% of subsidiary revenue before allocating profit to NCI. [AR26 AOC-1 and notes 1, 48; RESIGN24]\\n\\n**Geography and customer economics:** Group domestic/export classification was ₹325.26/₹528.37 crore in FY26. This is not a production-location breakdown. The parent alone recorded approximately ₹90.06 crore exports on ₹93.99 crore revenue. The two disclosed large customers contributed ₹132.50 crore and ₹67.13 crore. The latter amount matches related-party Rise sales, but the first customer is not identified here. No concentration-adjusted contract tenure or customer-retention cohort is available. [AR26 notes 30, 41, 45]\\n\\nManagement says RLD customers make 25–50% advances and stocks may be held until requested. That can improve funding, but only while advances, delivery acceptance and collections remain reliable. Customer advances actually rose from about ₹27.99 crore to ₹44.66 crore in FY26, while receivables and inventory rose substantially. Neither the verbal advance policy nor the reported advance liability removes the need to pay suppliers before receipt of final cash. [CALL26; AR26 notes 11, 13, 22, 24]\\n\\n## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\\n**Mechanisms that may persist:** a reusable dossier and registration organisation, long-standing local distributors, knowledge of country approvals and a supplier network can reduce launch friction. Reliable sourcing of scarce comparator drugs can attract repeat research customers. These are capabilities, not automatically exclusive rights. The dossiers must convert into approved products, commercial launches, repeat orders and collected cash; revenue growth alone does not prove durable pricing power.\\n\\n**Registration ladder, not a TAM slide:** FY25 materials referred to 620+ registered products, 400+ commercialised products and 745+ dossiers. FY26 materials referred to 903+ approvals, 1,400+ under evaluation, 2,243+ dossier registrations, 850+ ready dossiers, 430+ commercialised products and 2,100+ SKUs. These are management categories with different denominators. They are not additive unique molecules and 430/903 is not a valid approval-to-sales cohort conversion rate. No regulator-verified product-by-country list with approval date, expiry, ownership, launch sales and contribution margin was obtained. Therefore registration counts support access capability, but not a high-confidence regulatory monopoly. [DECK26, CALL25, CALL26]\\n\\nManagement reported a cleared Peru DIGEMID audit and planned filings, Saudi tender/registration efforts and semaglutide-related filings. Audit, submission, tender eligibility, approval, commercial launch and collected revenue are separate milestones. This research assigns no unearned revenue to any of those milestones. FDA guidance explicitly distinguishes registration from approval, and wholesale-distribution authorisation requires appropriate state licensure and reporting. The one exact historical Oklahoma record located for Espee Biopharma & Finechem LLC is **closed**, ending September 2022, with no disciplinary entries shown. That is **not evidence that its current US operations are unlicensed**, nor is it evidence of current authorisation elsewhere. Current jurisdiction-by-jurisdiction licences remain an open diligence item. Unrelated similarly named UK and Peruvian entities were excluded. [FDA, FDA-LICENSE, OK-LICENSE]\\n\\n**Economic comparator set, not a peer-multiple shortcut:**\\n\\n|Comparator|Relevant mechanism|Why it is not interchangeable with Remus|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|Lincoln Pharmaceuticals|Export formulations and product registration|Own manufacturing means different capital intensity, quality-control responsibilities and margins.|\\n|Marksans Pharma|Regulated-market formulations, distribution and manufacturing integration|Owned overseas/Indian manufacturing and product exposure differ from RLD comparator procurement.|\\n|Caplin Point Laboratories|Latin American front-end distribution plus formulations|Owned manufacturing, clinical capabilities and US injectable activity differ from Remus's current segment mix.|\\n|Senores Pharmaceuticals|Regulated-market manufacturing and dossiers; Remus investee|A related financial investment, not an independent valuation benchmark. Using its market multiple for Remus operations would double count the stake and obscure different ownership claims.|\\n\\nOfficial company materials support the first three structural comparisons. No comparable listed Indian pure-play RLD distributor with reconciled segment returns was established, so no fabricated peer median P/E, ROIC or market share is used. The moat score stays 5: useful access and execution capabilities, exposed to supplier concentration, regulatory interruption, customer substitution and financing capacity. [PEER-LINCOLN, PEER-MARKSANS, PEER-CAPLIN]\\n\\n## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\\n|Scope and interval|Start revenue ₹cr|End revenue ₹cr|Years|CAGR|Interpretation|\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\\n|Standalone FY20–FY26|11.9190|93.99|6|41.08%|Long-run original-business history, with accounting-basis transition; not the current group CAGR.|\\n|Standalone FY21–FY26|18.4181|93.99|5|38.54%|Useful five-year original-business scale signal, not a like-for-like consolidated cohort.|\\n|Standalone FY23–FY26|45.0273|93.99|3|27.80%|FY23 original basis and gratuity qualification disclosed.|\\n|Standalone FY24–FY26|63.94|93.99|2|21.24%|Later Ind AS comparatives; strongest recent parent comparison.|\\n|Consolidated FY24–FY26|212.94|853.63|2|100.22%|**Acquisition/full-year consolidation distortion. Not organic CAGR.**|\\n|Current consolidated group, five years|NS|NS|5|NS|No five-year same-scope history.|\\n\\nStandalone annual revenue, FY20–FY26, was ₹11.9190, 18.4181, 24.7171, 45.0273, 63.94, 79.16 and 93.99 crore. Annual additions were ₹6.4991, 6.2990, 20.3102, 18.9127, 15.22 and 14.83 crore respectively. The FY24 basis change means the ₹18.9127 crore bridge is not a pure volume/price bridge. Group additions were ₹407.42 crore in FY25 and ₹233.27 crore in FY26. [IPO, AR23–AR26]\\n\\nEspee's financial consolidation begins in FY24, with only a partial year; the definitive acquisition announcement was February 2024. It was a related-party cash acquisition, USD2.7 million, not internally generated growth. FY25 therefore captures a full-year scope step-up. By FY26, RLD revenue grew from ₹522.50 crore to ₹740.46 crore, about **41.7%**, while formulations grew from ₹69.37 crore to ₹93.48 crore, about **34.8%**. That supports existing-scope momentum, but a constant-currency price/volume/customer bridge is absent. The clinical startup had no FY26 sales; it is not the explanation for the growth. Do not classify all FY26 growth as rigorously proven organic growth. [AR24–AR26; RESIGN24]\\n\\nRecent half-years are **H1FY26 ₹400.20 crore and H2FY26 ₹453.43 crore**, versus H1FY25 ₹272.61 crore and H2FY25 ₹347.75 crore. Those halves sum to their annual totals. September 2026 earnings were not available by the cutoff, so there is no invented FY27 H1 or TTM roll-forward. Strong revenue performance coexists with a shrinking reported operating margin as low-margin RLD grows; scale is not a substitute for per-share cash generation. [FY26, H1FY26]\\n\\nREV=8 uses the documented original-business growth and recent existing-scope progress, with an explicit acquisition/history ceiling. It does **not** award a 100%-CAGR growth score to the bought revenue base.\\n\\n## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\\nThe practical scaling chain is: select product and country; secure compliant manufacturing and dossier rights; fund development and registration; win approval and distributor access; fund stock and receivables; collect cash; reinvest only the owner's share. Dossiers and SKUs are inputs to this process, not a measured addressable market. A bottom-up revenue forecast needs launches × sell-through × net realisation × retained customer share, plus repeat RLD demand. These unit cohorts are not disclosed, so scenario growth rates remain assumptions rather than an order-backed forecast.\\n\\n**Demand access versus delivery:** management targeted an increasing B2C contribution in FY25 commentary. The FY26 call reported only roughly 14–15% of non-RLD business, not of total consolidated revenue, versus a May 2025 management ambition of 25–30%; May 2026 questioners separately recalled an 18–20% target. Bolivia political/logistics delays were cited. FY26 added four named markets rather than automatically meeting an aspiration of five or more every year. Management cited 26 Bolivia launches and plans for another 40; these planned launches are not booked revenue. [CALL25, CALL26]\\n\\n**Capital is not negligible:** group receivables ₹139.84 crore plus inventory ₹72.06 crore are much larger than physical PPE. Clinical CWIP is ₹8.28 crore, with the new subsidiary financed by a parent loan around ₹9.58 crore. Continuing registrations, stock availability, export credit and supplier prepayments all consume capital. Acquiring a distribution platform can make accounting revenue rise quickly without establishing a high-return internally funded growth loop.\\n\\nThe broad invested-capital bridge in section 9 removes large financial investments and reconciles to operating capital, but there is no reliable segment/cohort bridge separating acquisition purchase price, goodwill, group transfers, operating advances, leased assets, currency translation and genuine incremental operating investment. Therefore **CAP=NS** despite apparently strong broad accounting returns. The model uses explicit growth-capital intensity, working-capital timing and minority claims; it does not reverse-engineer “asset light” into infinite growth at no cost.\\n\\nGQS remains NS, with the cash-conversion cap limiting any eventual numeric result to 5 on current evidence. To lift that cap, growth must produce sustained group cash conversion, not merely parent-only CFO or investment revaluation. Runway plausibly extends for several years, but duration is conditioned on regulatory continuity, collection performance and affordable capital, hence DUR=6.\\n\\n## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\\n**Audit history is not uniformly clean.** The IPO restatement report, printed F2–F3, expressly records qualified original FY20, FY21 and FY22 opinions because long-term employee benefits had been accounted for on a cash basis rather than accrued. Annexure 29 incorporates retrospective actuarial adjustments into the restated numbers. Printed F4 expressly distinguishes the restatement exercise from reissuing or redating the original opinions. Those old original audit reports were not separately obtained; the qualification history is supported firsthand by the signed restatement report, not inferred. FY23's audit was genuinely qualified on gratuity accounting: current-year expense of ₹10.22 lakh and an opening reserve adjustment of ₹19.51 lakh were at issue. The IPO restatement and later Ind AS statements are different vintages; their adjustments do not erase the original opinion. FY24's main opinion reads unmodified, while its basis paragraph refers to a qualified opinion and its other-matter paragraph acknowledges the prior modified opinion. This is an internal wording inconsistency, not a licence to label the whole history clean or to allege fraud. [AR23, AR24, IPO]\\n\\nParikh & Majmudar resigned effective 6 February 2024; Pankaj R. Shah and Associates succeeded them. The signed resignation letter and annexure state the reason as avoiding a potential future conflict of interest, with information-withholding concerns marked not applicable. The evidence does **not** support an allegation that the resignation resulted from obstruction or undisclosed wrongdoing. FY26's current audit opinion is unmodified, with revenue recognition a key audit matter; the standalone CARO and internal-financial-control reports do not report fraud/default or ineffective controls. These are positive assurance facts but do not guarantee recoverability of every balance. [RESIGN24; AR26 audit and annexures]\\n\\n**Assurance perimeter:** two small foreign subsidiaries were included using management-certified figures, approximately assets ₹11.80 crore, revenue ₹9.34 crore, PAT ₹3.06 crore and cash outflow ₹.15 crore. Two other subsidiaries were audited by other auditors, approximately revenue ₹755.79 crore and PAT ₹19.27 crore. The main consolidated report relies on those auditors for that work. Note 55 also states that confirmations of supplier, customer, loan and advance balances were awaited at approval, with book balances used. This is a material diligence limitation, not proof of fictitious balances. [AR26 consolidated audit, note 55]\\n\\n**Minority and earnings reconciliation:** main FY26 P&L total PAT ₹46.17 crore splits into owners ₹36.99 crore and NCI ₹9.18 crore. Reported EPS ₹39.19 corresponds to total PAT, whereas owner EPS on current shares is **₹31.39**. FY25 has the same issue: total PAT ₹38.42 crore versus owners ₹29.07 crore. Note 48 and AOC-1 show subsidiary profit/NCI figures that do not cleanly reconcile to the main allocation; for example, Espee profit ₹13.24 crore/NCI ₹6.49 crore differs from the main NCI and external-auditor perimeter. Do not calibrate a US segment margin from whichever inconsistent number gives the best valuation. Use main owner PAT for observed P/E and disclosed economic ownership only as an explicit forward proxy. [AR26 pp138–141, AOC-1, notes 48–49]\\n\\n**Related parties and allocation:** FY26 Rise sales ₹67.13 crore and purchases ₹10.19 crore sit within the separately disclosed ₹100 crore transaction ceiling; this report does not allege a cap breach. Rise receivables fell from ₹25.51 crore to ₹7.58 crore, which is a positive movement but not proof of collection of every outstanding related balance. Other items include Ratnatris purchases ₹8.35 crore, Senores development ₹2.14 crore, Renosen consultancy ₹7.37 crore, Espee Therapeutics ₹3.58 crore and Shashin Shah consultancy ₹4.41 crore. KMP remuneration and other benefits are separately disclosed. The operating rationale, pricing, recoverability and allocation to listed shareholders warrant continued review. Detailed related-party loan totals and presentation categories also need reconciliation rather than casual addition. [AR26 note 41; BALLOT25]\\n\\n**Capital allocation:** the Ratnatris stake exchange produced 3,261,744 Senores shares. The investment is carried through OCI, not operating PAT. A large rise in equity from investment marking is not evidence of cash reinvestment returns. IPO proceeds of ₹47.69 crore were reported fully used by March 2025, including working capital ₹30.31 crore, acquisitions/general purposes ₹16.38 crore and issue costs ₹1 crore. Reported utilisation is not proof that every resulting investment has met its hurdle. Historical guarantee/security exposure, about ₹75.35 crore in FY24, is not carried forward as a current obligation because later FY25 disclosures state nil and FY26 does not repeat it. However, exact current release/encumbrance evidence remains necessary before treating the listed stake as freely available cash. [AR24–AR26]\\n\\n|Dated statement or decision|Observed result by cutoff|Assessment|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|February 2024 Espee acquisition, USD2.7m, related-party|RLD became the dominant revenue stream; partial-year to full-year consolidation materially changed scale|Growth achieved, but compare owner cash and acquisition returns, not just sales.|\\n|May 2025 B2C ambition 25–30%; May 2026 questioners recalled 18–20%|FY26 call about 14–15% of non-RLD revenue|Below prior ambition; denominator must not become total group revenue.|\\n|Pipeline registrations and country expansion|Larger claimed pipeline; four named new markets in FY26; regulator-level conversion register unavailable|Partial operating progress, not all milestones independently verified.|\\n|FY26 clinical research expansion|CWIP and loans increased, startup remained pre-revenue|Capital deployed ahead of economic proof.|\\n|September 2026 main-board migration proposal|Shareholder approval reported 7 October; 19 October master still active SM|Approval is not completion and does not remove SME liquidity risk.|\\n\\nGOV=5 and EXEC=5 balance real growth and current assurance against these unresolved accounting, related-party and delivery issues. The May 2026 presentation reports March 31 ownership of promoters 71.0%, foreign portfolio investors 5.3%, alternative investment funds .8% and other public 22.9%. The April 2, 2026 promoter declaration reports nil encumbrance at March 31 and no encumbrance created in FY26; this is historical, not a current-date zero-pledge assurance. Latest promoter changes and insider transactions still require a cutoff-matched exchange update. The FY26 board report also records Anjali Shah’s CFO departure effective July 8, 2026 and Bhavik Shah’s appointment from July 9, approved June 23. No inference of wrongdoing is drawn from that transition. [AR26 board report; DECK26; PLEDGE26]\\n\\n## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\\nAll figures below are ₹ crore; consolidated FY24 is the later comparable Ind AS vintage.\\n\\n|Year|Revenue|Owner PAT|Total PAT|CFO|Reported capex|FCF|Recurring EBIT|Broad IC|\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n|FY24|212.94|20.52|24.28|1.35|3.29|-1.94|26.07|39.43|\\n|FY25|620.36|29.07|38.42|6.98|3.98|3.00|34.73|62.00|\\n|FY26|853.63|36.99|46.17|18.16|14.57|3.59|39.71|93.21|\\n\\n**Parent-only history, kept separate from the acquired consolidated group:**\\n\\n|Standalone year / basis|Revenue|PAT|CFO|Reported capex|FCF proxy|Borrowings|Cash as reported|\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n|FY20 / Indian GAAP, IPO restated|11.9190|0.8116|1.3026|0.5592|0.7434|0.3593|1.1981|\\n|FY21 / Indian GAAP, IPO restated|18.4181|1.0258|2.8379|0.0664|2.7715|0.2789|0.8305|\\n|FY22 / Indian GAAP, IPO restated|24.7171|3.3857|5.0378|0.0710|4.9668|0.1917|1.1486|\\n|FY23 / Indian GAAP; gratuity-qualified original; not silently restated|45.0273|8.5028|5.3140|2.7316|2.5824|7.9500|3.0382|\\n|FY24 / Ind AS FY25 comparative restatement|63.9400|16.6200|6.3100|3.5900|2.7200|1.4400|1.7100|\\n|FY25 / Ind AS|79.1600|19.9000|15.5900|3.9500|11.6400|2.1400|3.4600|\\n|FY26 / Ind AS|93.9900|24.7100|25.5000|3.9200|21.5800|4.3300|2.7600|\\n\\nThe FY20–FY22 figures are IPO-restated observations, not evidence that the original audits were unqualified. FY23 is the original Indian-GAAP vintage, including cash/bank deposits in its reported cash definition; the later transition comparison separates approximately ₹1.01 crore cash equivalents and ₹2.03 crore other bank balances. It is not an unrestricted-cash series. FY24–FY26 use the stated Ind AS scope. Original and restated vintages remain separately identifiable in the inputs; none is spliced into a current-group five-year CAGR. Calculated trading gross profit and EBITDA, including recurring versions that remove forfeitures and non-operating income where appropriate, are retained in historical_metrics.json rather than represented as company-reported segment margins.\\n\\n**Revenue → earnings → cash.** FY26 PBT ₹59.16 crore plus finance ₹1.47 crore gives reported EBIT ₹60.63 crore. Remove other income ₹6.69 crore and advance forfeitures ₹14.23 crore to obtain **₹39.71 crore recurring EBIT**, approximately 4.73% of recurring revenue ₹839.40 crore. This is a research normalization, not a company-reported segment result. Owner PAT is not obtained simply by applying a generic 49% deduction to all earnings. The historical owner-core-PAT diagnostic removes 75% of forfeitures and other income from owner PAT, but it is deliberately not used as a definitive owner profit forecast because entity allocation is unresolved.\\n\\nFY26 cash flow starts with PBT ₹59.16 crore, reaches approximately ₹61.30 crore before working-capital movements after depreciation, interest, finance and other adjustments, and then absorbs approximately ₹31.81 crore into receivables and ₹20.50 crore into inventory. Payables contributed ₹4.76 crore and other liabilities ₹17.32 crore; other asset/liability movements complete reported cash generated ₹27.62 crore. Tax paid ₹9.46 crore leaves **CFO ₹18.16 crore**. Cash investing outflow was ₹15.03 crore and financing inflow ₹1.47 crore; net change ₹4.59 crore reconciles ₹7.23 crore opening to ₹11.82 crore closing cash. Component rounding is retained rather than forced into a fictitious adjustment. [AR26 cash-flow statement and notes]\\n\\nGroup CFO/PAT is approximately **24.33% over FY24–FY26**, with total CFO ₹26.49 crore against total PAT ₹108.87 crore. Aggregate reported capex was ₹21.84 crore and FCF ₹4.65 crore. Parent standalone CFO improved to ₹25.50 crore in FY26 against PAT ₹24.71 crore, but parent cash performance cannot replace group cash generation when most consolidated revenue and a large NCI claim sit elsewhere. CASH=3 reflects persistent group conversion weakness, not a claim that every subsidiary is cash-loss-making.\\n\\n**Lease presentation, preserved and normalized separately:** reported FY26 capex/cash purchase line ₹14.57 crore includes leasehold assets, while financing includes a positive lease-liability movement about ₹1.85 crore. Note 21 shows non-cash lease additions ₹2.57 crore, cash rental ₹.95 crore and interest ₹.24 crore. Removing the non-cash addition from both sides suggests cash capex around **₹12.00 crore** and lease principal around **₹.71 crore**, within rounding, rather than genuine cash funding of ₹1.85 crore. Reported FCF remains ₹3.59 crore; diagnostic capex-adjusted FCF is about ₹6.16 crore **before separately classifying lease principal**, not a second free-cash benefit. Gross ROU depreciation ₹.86 crore less ₹.19 crore capitalised explains net ₹.67 crore; gross total depreciation ₹2.94 crore versus P&L ₹2.76 crore is substantially explained by that capitalization and rounding. It is not left as an unexplained audit discrepancy. [AR26 notes 5, 21, 36]\\n\\n**Working capital and capital returns:** broad invested capital is defined as owner equity + NCI + borrowings + lease liabilities − cash − other bank balances − deposits − financial investments − loans. This gives approximately **₹39.43 crore, ₹62.00 crore and ₹93.21 crore** in FY24, FY25 and FY26. It includes goodwill and operating leased assets, and uses carrying values. Recurring EBIT ×75% is a standardized NOPAT proxy; dividing by average broad IC gives roughly 51% in FY25 and 38% in FY26. The incremental NOPAT/ΔIC proxy is much lower and volatile, roughly 29% then 12%. The operating side is net operating working capital plus PPE/intangibles, CWIP, intangible development, goodwill and ROU, plus other non-current operating assets: ₹53.25/₹89.33/₹135.63 crore. Financing minus operating capital is −₹13.82/−₹27.33/−₹42.42 crore, fully reconciled at disclosed rounding to non-deposit financial assets less non-advance current liabilities, provisions and current/deferred tax. The arithmetic residual is zero, but allocating deferred tax between financial investments and operations is not solved by that identity. Average operating ROIC proxies are 36.54%/26.48% for FY25/FY26, materially below the financial-asset-stripped broad ratios. Exact calculations, average owner ROE and total-capital ROCE are retained in historical_metrics.json. Those figures are **not verified segment/cohort returns**: acquisition scope, non-cash adjustments, investment stripping, OCI tax and customer-advance effects matter. They support further diligence, not a numeric CAP score.\\n\\nReceivable/revenue days at year-end are about 198, 64 and 60 for FY24–FY26; the FY24 denominator contains only a partial US year, so its apparent improvement is materially distorted. Inventory/revenue and payable/revenue days are disclosed as proxies only. Separately, reported purchases plus inventory-expense change gives trading COGS ₹163.37/₹532.87/₹742.21 crore; the calculation files include closing and average-inventory COGS days and average-payables/purchase days, with the partial-year FY24 limitation retained. FY26 receivables ₹139.84 crore plus stock ₹72.06 crore, less payables ₹76.04 crore and customer advances ₹44.66 crore, leave about **₹91.20 crore** before other operating balances. Even a modest collection delay can overwhelm cash ₹11.82 crore.\\n\\n**Liquidity, debt and assets:** FY26 borrowings ₹23.04 crore comprise ₹17.37 crore due within a year and ₹5.67 crore later; demand working-capital borrowing is approximately ₹17.04 crore. Leases total ₹4.03 crore, including ₹1.01 crore current. The maturity table divides borrowings ₹17.37/₹4.64/₹1.03 crore and leases ₹1.01/₹2.67/₹.35 crore across the disclosed buckets. Current deposits ₹24.50 crore are the current portion of financial-institution deposits with original maturity over 12 months, **not cash equivalents**. Other bank balances ₹1.06 crore, loans ₹9.65 crore, mutual funds ₹10.32 crore and AIF ₹7.21 crore are classified separately. Remaining disclosed capital commitments are about ₹.57 crore. No current credit rating or unused committed line was independently established. [AR26 notes 9, 12, 14, 20, 21, 42, 45]\\n\\n**Senores investment:** 3,261,744 shares cost about ₹20.55 crore and were marked at ₹257.03 crore at March 2026. Total FY26 financial investments were ₹274.56 crore. The October 19 official Senores close ₹1,253.60 implies **₹408.89 crore** for the disclosed share count, subject to no subsequent stake change. The holding quantity after March and current encumbrance are not proven by a price quote. FY26 OCI included a substantial fair-value gain; approximately ₹69.70 crore gross investment uplift and ₹60.15 crore total OCI explain much of equity growth without funding operations. The model neither treats OCI as EBIT nor adds both the stake value and capitalised investment earnings.\\n\\n**Per-share and foreign-currency discipline:** July 2025 1:1 and the earlier 3:1 bonus changed denominators, not economic ownership. Current issued shares are 11,784,000; authorised capital corresponds to 12,500,000 shares, leaving a modeled hard ceiling of 716,000 additional shares unless a future authorization occurs. No prospective authorization is assumed. Currency exposure notes disclose a net USD exposure equivalent to ₹15.91 crore and euro exposure around ₹.39 crore, but this is not a complete hedge or gross US-revenue exposure analysis. Translation, pricing currency, procurement currency and blocked/repatriable cash are different risks; the model stresses margins and available cash rather than assuming a fully natural hedge. [AR26 capital and risk notes; MASTER]\\n\\n## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\\n**Observed entry metrics:** market cap ₹1160.13 crore; corrected owner EPS ₹31.39; owner P/E **31.36x**. The lower **25.13x** total-PAT multiple is not the shareholder's earnings multiple. Neither a group EV/EBITDA multiple that ignores NCI nor a P/E after subtracting the entire unencumbered stake at spot is used as a purchase rule. The financial holding and two operating engines require an explicit sum-of-parts bridge.\\n\\n### SME execution reality\\n\\nThe 60 official sessions from 24 July through 19 October show mean daily traded value ₹1,02,58,814 and median ₹50,73,552.50. The last 20 sessions show mean ₹94,15,796.50 and median ₹39,31,495, with no missing or zero-turnover observations. At the lower of the 60-day mean and median, a ₹5 lakh exit needs theoretical 4.9275 / 1.9710 / .9855 sessions at 2% / 5% / 10% participation. Whole-lot capacity uses each historical session's actual traded **shares**, not rupees divided by today's price: at least one 100-share lot fits on 34 / 57 / 60 of the 60 sessions respectively. At entry, one lot costs ₹98,450, five lots ₹4,92,250 and six ₹5,90,700. Fractions of a session, historical turnover and a quoted close do not guarantee a fill. No position-size or exit promise is inferred from this reference.\\n\\nThe framework starts at L=9 for the theoretical 1.971-day figure. The explicit judgment adjustment is **9→7**, reflecting observed concentration (the top five sessions supply 41.85% of 60-day turnover), the lower recent 20-day median and indivisible lots. Median 5%-participation capacity is two lots. Completed rolling exits take two sessions median for five lots and three for six, with maxima five/six and two right-censored end-windows. Unknown spreads/circuits are limitations, not observed adverse evidence. [TAPE, MASTER]\\n\\n### Cash-linked scenario construction\\n\\nThe executable inputs start with audited **31 March 2026 cash ₹11.82 crore**, borrowings ₹23.04 crore and disclosed working-capital balances. Seven pre-entry periods total **202 days** through October 19. Those are projected movements, not unaudited actual cash. No pre-entry refinancing, stock sale or issue is permitted; current issued shares must reconcile to the exchange master. The announced dividend is deducted in the pre-entry bridge. After entry, 60 monthly periods give five years, followed by 12 continuation months to test terminal operating feasibility.\\n\\nEach period records revenue, cash operating cost, target receivables/inventory/payables/advances, gradual balance transition, collections, supplier cash, tax, interest, cash leases, capex, principal and shares. Supplier cash is paid **before** receipts within each cash cycle; period-end cash alone is insufficient. The main case uses **8 cycles per month**, with 1/2/4/8/12/16/24/32 alternatives. This is an explicit timing assumption, not measured supplier credit. The frequency16 illustration is shown separately because changing cash timing materially changes feasibility; it does not replace or conceal the failed main case.\\n\\nOperating margins are before cash lease rent but after physical depreciation. Physical depreciation is .249% of revenue, based on approximate net non-ROU depreciation, and cash rent ₹.95 crore annually is deducted once. Tax is 25% of positive earnings after cash rent and interest, an assumption rather than entity-specific tax advice. Maintenance capex is .4% of sales; growth fixed investment is 3% of the **rolling prior-year revenue increment**; clinical completion spending is separately modeled. Working-capital balances move gradually toward scenario days/advance ratios. Demand debt is repaid in modeled post-entry month five, approximately March 2027, rather than silently rolled over, and other principal is amortised. New debt incurs interest beginning the following monthly period; higher-rate sensitivities expose that convention rather than treating it as free finance.\\n\\n|Assumption|Bear|Base|Bull|\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n|RLD annual growth|2%|12%|20%|\\n|Other recurring revenue growth|8%|18%|25%|\\n|RLD EBIT margin before cash rent|1.8%|2.5%|3.2%|\\n|Other EBIT margin before cash rent|18%|21.5%|27%|\\n|Receivable days target|85|60|50|\\n|Inventory / payable days target|45 / 30|34 / 34|28 / 36|\\n|Customer-advance / annual sales target|3.5%|5.3%|6.5%|\\n|Senores price growth / terminal haircut|−3% / 35%|6% / 25%|10% / 15%|\\n|Additional currency margin drag|.3 percentage points|0|0|\\n\\n**Financing modes are separate:** no-new-finance permits no refinancing, equity or discretionary asset monetisation. Deposit maturities are not treated as committed because exact dates are absent. Bounded-optional permits, only after entry, finite mutual-fund/deposit sales with a 1% realization haircut, then up to ₹15 crore incremental bank debt, then a **whole-share issue**, priced at 25% below entry with 3% issue cost. New issue count cannot exceed 716,000, authorised headroom, or ₹50 crore net cash raised. These are alternative possibilities, **not approved or committed funding**. Unspent investments are retained in terminal value; sold amounts cannot be counted again. No Senores sale funds a rescue. A dedicated stress removes liquid financial assets and new debt so that the whole-share mechanism is tested at zero, 100,000 and 716,000-share caps.\\n\\n**First breach is absorbing:** every pre-entry, intra-period, annual and terminal-proof cash event is tested against a ₹2 crore minimum. Once breached, ordinary CAGR and terminal equity return become **null/NS**. The engine retains the unpaid/failing path and shortfall but never manufactures a normal return on an unfunded operating plan. A terminal-proof failure also removes the ordinary return. A scenario breach is not proof that the real issuer ran out of cash; it demonstrates missing timing/funding evidence.\\n\\n|Scenario / finance|Status|First breach|Conditional five-year CAGR|Issued shares|\\n|---|---|---|---:|---:|\\n|bear/no_new_finance|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre1|NS|0|\\n|bear/bounded_optional|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre1|NS|0|\\n|base/no_new_finance|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre2|NS|0|\\n|base/bounded_optional|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre2|NS|0|\\n|bull/no_new_finance|CONDITIONAL|None|5.61%|0|\\n|bull/bounded_optional|CONDITIONAL|None|5.61%|0|\\n|bear/frequency16/no_new_finance|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre1|NS|0|\\n|bear/frequency16/bounded_optional|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre1|NS|0|\\n|base/frequency16/no_new_finance|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|investment/Y1M06|NS|0|\\n|base/frequency16/bounded_optional|CONDITIONAL|None|-4.57%|0|\\n|bull/frequency16/no_new_finance|CONDITIONAL|None|5.70%|0|\\n|bull/frequency16/bounded_optional|CONDITIONAL|None|5.70%|0|\\n\\n### Earned terminal valuation, not assumed rerating\\n\\nTerminal year-six revenue grows at 4%; operating discount/capitalisation rates are 14% RLD and 13% other, stressed separately. FCFF is derived from the **same 12 continuation rows** used for the cash test: after-cash-rent unlevered NOPAT + physical depreciation − actual continuation capex − actual change in operating working capital. Shared capital/cash flows are allocated by revenue explicitly because segment cash data are absent. No positive FCFF or no valid k−g means no supported terminal. A cash reserve equal to the continuation path's maximum cumulative draw plus minimum cash is retained; **only cash above that reserve** is added to equity. Debt is deducted, economic minority claims are applied to segment equity, and lease debt is not deducted again after rent has already been charged.\\n\\nRLD economic NCI is 48.997%, other-business NCI proxy 1%, both assumptions tied to the ownership chain and tested. The cash/debt allocation of 80% to RLD is also sensitivity-tested. This is not a claim that all subsidiaries can freely upstream cash. Add only the separately valued Senores holding after the stated haircut and 15% gain-tax reserve, plus unspent funds/deposits and explicitly impaired loan/AIF recoveries. The tax reserve is a conservative scenario assumption, not a determination of actual tax treatment. Future dividends are zero; all return measures are five-year price CAGR after modeled dilution.\\n\\n### Reverse expectations and sensitivities\\n\\nThe margin reverse test searches RLD EBIT margins **0–30%** with other base assumptions and frequency16 held fixed. Every scan and bisection trial is retained. It only bisects adjacent feasible sign-changing endpoints; any failed funding/terminal midpoint invalidates that bracket. No root is inferred across a cash-failure discontinuity. Bisection brackets and residuals are in reverse_results.json.\\n\\n|Target CAGR|Finance|RLD EBIT-margin root bracket|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|12.00%|no_new_finance|16.933938% to 16.933938%|\\n|12.00%|bounded_optional|16.933938% to 16.933938%|\\n|15.00%|no_new_finance|20.638201% to 20.638201%|\\n|15.00%|bounded_optional|20.638201% to 20.638201%|\\n|18.00%|no_new_finance|24.749833% to 24.749833%|\\n|18.00%|bounded_optional|24.749833% to 24.749833%|\\n\\nA separate revenue-growth scan covers **0–50% RLD growth** at fixed 2.5% RLD margin, without claiming continuity or an exact root across financing boundaries. The hurdle prices at 12%, 15% and 18% are computed exactly from ₹984.50×(1+hurdle)^5. These tests expose how much margin, growth and funding the entry price demands; they do not establish that the company can achieve it.\\n\\nOne-way tests cover opening available cash, collection days, inventory days, advances, cycle frequency, debt caps/rates, NCI, allocation of debt/cash, Senores growth/haircut, terminal growth and capitalisation rates, FX margin drag and rollover assumptions. Two-way tests cover RLD growth × margin and receivable days × terminal capitalisation rate. The whole-share rescue tests include insufficient-cap cases. All 312 unique paths and 502 aliases, including failed roots and duplicate evaluations, are preserved losslessly; paths.json.gz is a deduplicated path pool, not a summary that discards failures.\\n\\n**No supportable buy range is asserted.** A low model price in a failed path is not a bargain price, and a conditional positive return cannot overcome unresolved cash availability, minority and accounting gates. Any future entry range must be rebuilt with verified current cash, liabilities, contractual financing, ownership and a defensible capital allocation, then clear the hurdle without an unearned terminal multiple.\\n\\n## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\\nThe strongest bear case is not that revenue stops growing. It is that **thin-margin RLD growth consumes cash faster than suppliers and banks will fund it**, while the listed shareholder owns only about half of the US engine. Add delayed collection, slower inventory turnover, reduced customer advances and adverse currency economics. Cash fails before annual PAT reveals the problem; optional financing may be unavailable or dilutive; registration/clinical spending then competes with working capital. The model's early bear/base timing failures illustrate this mechanism without asserting an observed default.\\n\\nA second loss channel is investment concentration. A lower Senores price, a higher realization haircut, encumbrance or non-transferable ownership reduces the financial cushion. It is not appropriate to use a full spot stake value as immediate debt-repayment cash while also capitalising its future upside. A third is regulatory interruption at a CMO or distributor, causing approval delays, recalls, unusable inventory or loss of comparator supply. A fourth is poor related-party capital allocation that leaves listed shareholders with cash claims rather than distributable cash.\\n\\nPermanent impairment can arise from dilutive rescue, receivable write-offs, expired inventory, lost licences or an acquisition price/operating-cost structure that cannot earn its capital cost. A price drawdown alone is not permanent impairment; conversely, a large balance-sheet investment does not prevent operating liquidity failure. The **thesis-break variable is verified group cash generation available after minority claims and obligatory capital needs**, not a single revenue growth rate. No precise recovery value or downside score is fabricated for failed paths. D stays NS.\\n\\n## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\\nA five-year **5×** price requires approximately **37.97% annual appreciation** and a price of **₹4,922.50**; **10×** requires approximately **58.49%** and **₹9,845.00**. At unchanged shares those imply market caps of about ₹5,800.67 crore and ₹11,601.35 crore. At a hypothetical 20× owner P/E, required owner PAT would be about ₹290.03 crore and ₹580.07 crore; at 30×, ₹193.36 crore and ₹386.71 crore. These are algebraic hurdles, not target multiples.\\n\\nAgainst FY26 owner PAT ₹36.99 crore, the required earnings growth is very substantial, before accounting for dilution, minority claims and capital funding. Group revenue growing rapidly at a 2–3% RLD operating margin does not automatically create those owner earnings. A multi-bagger case must demonstrate expansion of higher-margin formulations or durable improvement in RLD economics, repeatable cash conversion, sufficient capital and a supportable terminal value. The current dossier and commercialisation counts do not establish this. No 5×/10× probability is assigned, and no acquired-revenue CAGR is extrapolated to manufacture one.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\\n|KPI and baseline|Evidence needed / next milestone|Warning or thesis-break threshold|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|Owner EPS ₹31.39 versus reported ₹39.19 FY26|Company/auditor reconciliation of EPS numerator, bonus denominator, AOC-1 and Note48|Unresolved after next audited cycle, or further inconsistencies: keep G2 Hold.|\\n|Group CFO/PAT 24.3% over three years|Next two reporting periods and rolling three-year bridge; reconcile parent/subsidiary cash|Rolling conversion below50% while high growth persists is a warning; negative available cash without committed funding breaks investability.|\\n|Receivables ₹139.84cr and inventory ₹72.06cr|Ageing, subsequent receipts, inventory expiry and purchase obligations by entity|DSO >75 or material >180-day receivables without recoverability evidence; investigate rather than mechanically accept sales.|\\n|Advances ₹44.66cr; forfeiture income ₹14.23cr|Separate recurring sales from releases of historical advances|Profit growth dependent on forfeitures while customer advances decline undermines earnings quality.|\\n|Demand borrowing about ₹17.04cr|Renewal letters, covenants, unused committed lines and currency/entity restrictions|Renewal absent near maturity or inability to maintain a reasonable intraperiod cash buffer.|\\n|RLD ₹740.46cr; effective parent interest51.003%|Audited segment cash margin and shareholder-distribution reconciliation|Top-line growth without incremental owner cash; minority leakage larger than modeled.|\\n|903+ approvals /430+ commercialised, management categories|Product-country register, regulator status, launch dates and sales by cohort|Expiry, rejection or commercialisation slippage; no automatic credit for filing counts.|\\n|B2C about14–15% of non-RLD revenue|Like-for-like FY27 share, margins, cash costs and country milestones|Another miss against own targets without denominator-consistent explanation.|\\n|Senores3,261,744shares, Marchholding|Latest ownership/encumbrance and investment-allocation policy|Stake unavailable for holders, loss beyond haircut assumptions, or new related-party deployment without clear returns.|\\n|SMlot100; median60-session turnover₹50.74lakh|Updated official tape, spread/depth/circuit/series facts before action|Entry/exit not feasible within conservative participation; migration proposal alone does not cure it.|\\n\\nThese are research triggers, not a commitment to trade automatically. Update after the next financial release, material acquisition/financing, auditor clarification or listing-series change, using a new explicit cutoff.\\n\\n## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\\n**Classification: Hold–unresolved; not ranked.** BQS/GQS/IAS remain NS. This is not a “high-quality but expensive” label with a hidden numeric score; evidence gaps prevent that classification. It is also not a proven fraud, default or worthless business. The original business grew substantially, current audits contain meaningful assurance, and the investment stake is economically material. Those positives coexist with weak group conversion, minority-heavy distribution earnings and unresolved accounting/financing detail.\\n\\nThe compounder label is withheld because durable high incremental returns, self-funded growth, clean owner earnings and adequate entry return have not all been demonstrated. No buy/avoid trade instruction or portfolio weight is produced from the present conditional models. Price matters, but a lower quote alone cannot fix unclear cash claims. Reconsider after (1) reconciled EPS/subsidiary allocations, (2) verified legal-entity cash and contractual finance, (3) cash-converting product/customer cohorts, and (4) a cash-feasible owner return comfortably above the hurdle at a new price. Until then, the action is diligence/watchlist rather than ranking with fully underwritten companies.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\\n### Source ledger\\n\\nSource IDs used above map to official documents and exact reviewed sections in source_register.json. Annual-report printed pages are used where available; the FY26 PDF contains facing-page spreads, so PDF page number and printed page differ. The working papers retain numerical transcriptions and public-source references, not a republished annual report.\\n\\n- **AR26:** [FY2025–26 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Annual-Report-FY-2025-2026.pdf), September 2026; standalone audit/CARO/IFC, consolidated opinion/other matters, full statements and notes, especially 5, 9–24, 30, 36, 41–45, 48–55. Main group statements printed 138–141; subsidiary and allocation tables separately checked.\\n- **AR25:** [FY2024–25 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Annual-Report-FY-2024-2025.pdf), September 2025; both audit reports/annexures, statements, restatement/transition, acquisition, investments, related parties, IPO use and guarantee disclosures.\\n- **AR24:** [FY2023–24 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual-Report-FY-2023-2024.pdf), August 2024; opinion wording, CARO/resignation, financials, acquisition and guarantee schedules. Original vintage retained separately rather than mixed into later comparatives.\\n- **AR23:** [FY2022–23 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/Annual-Report-2022-2023.pdf), August 2023; original qualified audit and gratuity treatment, statements and notes.\\n- **IPO:** [Final prospectus](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/Prospectus.pdf), May 2023; identity, capital, risk factors, business, objects, restatement report and financial annexures. Old history is not the present consolidated group.\\n- **FY26:** [May 2026 audited results](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/FINANCIAL-RESULTS-FOR-HALF-YEAR-AND-YEAR-ENDED-MARCH-31-2026.pdf), 20 May 2026; both audit opinions, annual and half-year comparisons.\\n- **H1FY26:** [September 2025 half-year results](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/UNAUDITED-FINANCIAL-RESULTS-FOR-HALF-YEAR-ENDED-30.09.2025.pdf), 10 November 2025; limited-review period only, not September 2026.\\n- **CALL25 / CALL26:** [FY25 call](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Investors-Conference-Call-Transcript-H2-FY25.pdf) and [FY26 call](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Investors-Conference-Call-Transcript-H2-FY26-FY26.pdf), May 2025/May 2026; management claims, targets and Q&A distinguished from audited facts.\\n- **DECK26:** [FY26 investor presentation](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Investor-Presentation-H2-FY26-FY26.pdf), May 2026; claimed registration/launch categories, not independent regulatory certifications.\\n- **RESIGN24:** [7 February 2024 board outcome and annexures](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/REMUS_07022024180724_Outcomeofboardmeetingsigned.pdf); acquisition price/related-party terms and signed auditor resignation reason.\\n- **MIGRATION26:** [7 October 2026 shareholder approval](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/REMUS_07102026191521_OutcomeofPostalBallotsigned.pdf); special resolution passed, not an exchange-effective migration.\\n- **BALLOT25 / BALLOT26:** [Rise transaction notice](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Notice-of-Postal-Ballot-10.11.2025.pdf) and [migration proposal](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Notice-of-Postal-Ballot-02.09.2026.pdf); ceilings/proposals do not prove completion or funding.\\n- **PLEDGE26:** [Promoter encumbrance declaration](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/team_bbodade_09042026155545_REMUS.pdf),2 April 2026, available 9 April 2026; nil atMarch 31 only.\\n- **MASTER / TAPE:** [NSE security master, 19 October 2026](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz) and [official daily bhavcopy](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/BhavCopy_NSE_CM_0_0_0_20261019_F_0000.csv.zip). market_extract.json contains all 60 exact-issuer observations, original URLs and archive/content hashes, including day counts; investment_market_extract.json separately records the Senores quote.\\n- **FDA / FDA-LICENSE:** [Registration versus approval](https://www.fda.gov/consumers/consumer-updates/it-really-fda-approved) and [wholesale-distributor licence checking](https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-supply-chain-integrity/check-licensure-wholesale-drug-distributors-and-third-party-logistics-providers), 2026 guidance available before cutoff. Used for terminology, not an assertion of issuer approval.\\n- **OK-LICENSE:** [Exact historical Oklahoma Espee record](https://lv.pharmacy.ok.gov/FacilityDetail.asp?CountyTypeLic=88-W-4787), update dated 11 September 2026; closed historical licence, not a nationwide conclusion.\\n- **PEER-LINCOLN / PEER-MARKSANS / PEER-CAPLIN:** [Lincoln FY25 report](https://www.lincolnpharma.com/Investor/Disclosures%20under%20Regulation%2046%20of%20the%20LODR/15.%20Annual%20Reports/2024-25/Annual%20Report%20F_2024-25%20LPL.pdf), [Marksans FY25 report](https://www.marksanspharma.com/pdf/annual-report/marksans-annual-report-2024-2025.pdf), [Caplin structural business description](https://www.caplinpoint.net/index.php/our-business/). Mechanism comparisons only; no subsequent financial result or spot peer multiple imported.\\n\\n### Reconstructibility and model limits\\n\\nThe authored inputs are historical_inputs.json, model_inputs.json, score_inputs.json, source_register.json, framework_provenance.json, market_extract.json, security_extract.json, investment_market_extract.json and this report template. Standard-library build.py/model_engine.py/path_codec.py regenerate all research tables, exact scores, liquidity, full cash-event paths, aliases, sensitivities and reverse trials. verify.py independently checks arithmetic, ledger identities, share caps, absorbing breaches, terminal cash/capital alignment and reconstruction. guarded_rebuild.py removes generated outputs and old attestations before rebuilding with an explicit read/write allowlist and denied network/subprocess calls. The compact recovery archive contains every minimal input and executable dependency; the full archive adds generated outputs. Neither requires the original source PDFs, OCR, a helper installation, network access or a private path to recreate the research calculations.\\n\\n**Material assumptions are not facts:** cash pooling; supplier-before-collection frequency; working-capital target adjustment; growth/margin/tax; useful life/maintenance/growth capex; debt maturity timing and uncommitted alternatives; issue price/fees/headroom; minority allocation; terminal capitalisation rates and continuity; available investment holdings, market growth, realization haircut/tax and residual loan recovery. The scenario engine makes these falsifiable, not known. Source-safe extracts reproduce the entered numbers and provenance, but cannot recreate the issuer's undisclosed contracts or prove future cash.\\n\\n**Open questions in priority order:** (1) corrected EPS and subsidiary allocation reconciliation; (2) current cash, restrictions, balances and bank renewal by entity; (3) customer/supplier confirmations and subsequent collections; (4) clean non-cash lease cash-flow reconciliation; (5) product-country regulatory register and commercialisation economics; (6) segment/cohort incremental capital and owner distributions; (7) current Senores quantity, encumbrance and capital-allocation policy; (8) recent shareholding/pledges and live execution constraints. These gaps are carried as NS or conditional status, not quietly converted into favourable assumptions.\\n\\nResearch update: initial v3 dossier with cutoff 20 October 2026. No publication or trade is implied. Changes to sources, inputs, scoring or models require a new deterministic rebuild and independent review.\\n\"","\"# Remus Pharmaceuticals Limited\\n## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\\n**HOLD–UNRESOLVED. Not ranked; not an underwritten compounder.** Remus combines an outsourced-formulation export and registration business with a much larger, thin-margin US reference-listed-drug (RLD) distribution business acquired from promoter-related entities. The strength is genuine growth in the original business and access to international distribution; the weakness is the conversion of consolidated growth into cash available to the listed-company shareholder. At ₹984.50, market capitalisation is ₹1160.13 crore and corrected owner-earnings P/E is 31.36x, not the 25.13x obtained from total group PAT. A substantial Senores shareholding is valuable but is not operating cash or proof of a cheap stock. The unresolved EPS/minority reconciliations, poor group cash conversion and sensitivity to intra-period funding prevent a dependable entry valuation. The thesis is a **growth-plus-investment-holding special situation**, not a proven asset-light compounder. A cleaner legal-entity cash bridge and earnings reconciliation, followed by an adequately funded return above the hurdle, would change this conclusion. [AR26, AR25, RESIGN24, MASTER, TAPE]\\n\\n## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\\n- Exact issuer: **Remus Pharmaceuticals Limited**, CIN L24232GJ2015PLC084536, **ISIN INE0O5T01011**, NSE symbol REMUS. Do not confuse it with unrelated “Remus” pharmaceutical brands.\\n- Information cutoff: **20 October 2026, India time**. Market reference: **19 October 2026 official NSE closing price ₹984.50**. Source retrieval occurred later, but no later earnings, corporate action or market price enters the valuation.\\n- The active security is **SM / NSE EMERGE**. The September 2026 migration proposal received shareholder approval reported on 7 October, but that is not a completed main-board migration. Keep this research on the SME page. The exchange master supplies **11,784,000 issued shares** and **100-share lot**. Capitalisation is calculated with actual shares, not an unadjusted pre-bonus denominator.\\n- Scope: consolidated FY24–FY26 for the investable group; separately retained standalone FY20–FY26 for original-business history. FY24 is the later Ind AS comparative, not the unreconciled original Indian-GAAP vintage. Group five-year CAGR is **not available**.\\n- Horizon: five years from the reference entry, preceded by a projected 202-day audited-balance-sheet-to-entry bridge. Required return reference **15%**, with **12% and 18%** hurdles. ₹5 lakh liquidity reference is illustrative, not a stated user holding.\\n- **High confidence:** audited line-item transcriptions, exact security identity, corporate ownership chain and official daily trading observations. **Medium:** organic growth, underlying profitability and historical capital returns because of segment/perimeter changes and presentation issues. **Low:** current cash availability by legal entity, sustainable segment cash margins, current product-level approval register, financing commitments, and terminal realisability of investment assets.\\n\\n|Gate|Status|Reason and consequence|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|G1 Integrity / governance|Conditional, not Fail|No substantiated fraud or default finding; a real historical audit qualification, related-party acquisition and material RPT warrant scrutiny. Do not turn absence of an adverse finding into a clean bill of health.|\\n|G2 Accounting / evidence|Hold–unresolved|Reported EPS includes NCI earnings; subsidiary allocation tables do not reconcile cleanly; lease cash-flow classification and balance confirmations need clarification. Owner EPS can be corrected arithmetically, but segment economics cannot be repaired by guesswork.|\\n|G3 Funding / solvency|Conditional|Audited net assets are ample. However, demand borrowings, cash location and timing of collections matter. Optional refinancing, share issuance and investment sales are not committed liquidity. Model breaches describe assumptions, not an assertion of an actual company default.|\\n|G4 Investability / liquidity|Conditional|Active SME trading and lot size verified; spread, executable order book, current surveillance and binding circuit details not verified.|\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\\nThe actual v3.0 framework, updated 3 October 2026, is applied without substituting an earlier framework. Its SHA-256 is 68d4de4255427df276318d0231530b867a453e740c74150e9768bf1387224137. All arithmetic uses Decimal precision 50. Display rounding is half-up; no intermediate rounding or missing-factor renormalisation.\\n\\n|Factor|Score|Confidence|Evidence IDs and limitation|\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n|GOV|5|Medium|AR23, AR24, AR26, RESIGN24, PLEDGE26: Current unmodified audits and controls, but historical qualifications, cash/earnings presentation, related parties and minority reconciliation reduce confidence.|\\n|MOAT|5|Medium|AR26, CALL26, FDA, OK-LICENSE: Country registrations, distributor relationships and RLD sourcing capability; no patent or owned FDA-plant moat, and the current jurisdictional licence register is incomplete.|\\n|REV|8|Medium|IPO, AR23, AR25, AR26, FY26: Standalone revenue grew from ₹11.919 crore in FY20 to ₹93.99 crore in FY26, with recent existing-scope group growth. This is not a spliced acquired-group five-year CAGR; acquisition and denominator limits prevent 9/10.|\\n|DUR|6|Medium|CALL25, CALL26, DECK26: Generic penetration, recurring comparator-drug needs and dossiers provide runway, conditional on funding, currency, launch and regulatory execution.|\\n|CAP|NS|Low|AR24, AR25, AR26: No reliable cohort or legal-entity incremental operating-capital bridge after acquisition and valuation changes. The reconciled broad proxy is reported but insufficient for attribution.|\\n|CASH|3|High|AR25, AR26: FY24–FY26 aggregate group CFO ₹26.49 crore / PAT ₹108.87 crore = 24.33%. Large receivables and inventory cause persistent weak conversion despite parent improvement.|\\n|BAL|6|Medium|AR26, TAPE: Large financial assets versus modest borrowings, but investments are not free cash; demand debt, FX, entity transfer and historical guarantee risks remain.|\\n|EXEC|5|Medium|CALL25, CALL26, AR26: Revenue execution is strong. B2C 14–15% trails the May 2025 management ambition of 25–30% and the 18–20% target recalled by May 2026 questioners. Registration cohorts are unverified and the clinical unit is pre-revenue.|\\n|EARN|5|Medium|AR25, AR26: Owner earnings grow, but forfeitures and OCI need stripping. EPS uses total PAT including NCI and subsidiary allocations conflict.|\\n|R|NS|Low|AR26, model assumptions: Cash forecasts are not current verified balances; legal-entity minority and free-cash claims remain unresolved. Conditional scenario returns are not underwritable expected returns.|\\n|D|NS|Low|AR26, model assumptions: Cash-breach scenarios cannot have ordinary drawdown valuations. Realisability and entity claims remain unresolved.|\\n|P|NS|Low|AR26, MASTER, TAPE, model assumptions: Observed owner multiples are corrected, but cash-funded intrinsic value and ownership/asset realisation are not sufficiently reliable for a price-versus-value score.|\\n|L|7|Medium|MASTER, TAPE: Framework starting score 9 at 1.971 theoretical days, explicitly reduced to 7 for 100-share lots, top-five-session concentration of 41.85% and the lower 20-session median. Median 5% capacity is two whole lots; unknown spreads/circuits are not adverse facts.|\\n\\n|Composite|Known weighted sum|Coverage|Exact lower–upper before cap|Final|Postcap display bounds|Confidence|\\n|---|---:|---:|---|---|---|---|\\n|BQS|4.74|88.00%|4.86–5.94|NS|4.9–5.9|Low, missing factors|\\n|GQS|5.45|85.00%|5.60–6.95|NS|5.0–5.0|Low, missing factors|\\n|IAS|0.70|10.00%|2.5650–8.6850|NS|2.6–8.7|Low, missing factors|\\n\\n**Exact contribution audit:** BQS = GOV 5×.20 + MOAT 5×.20 + REV 8×.12 + DUR 6×.10 + CAP NS×.12 + CASH 3×.10 + BAL 6×.08 + EXEC 5×.08. Known subtotal 4.74, coverage 88%, raw interval **4.86–5.94**, final **NS**. GQS = REV 8×.40 + DUR 6×.20 + EARN 5×.15 + CAP NS×.15 + CASH 3×.10. Known subtotal 5.45, coverage 85%, raw interval **5.60–6.95**. Persistent poor cash conversion with CASH=3 caps the interval at **5–5**, but **does not make the missing composite a numeric 5**. Final remains NS. IAS uses final BQS×.25, R×.35, D×.20, P×.10 and L×.10. Only L is available at the outer level, so coverage is 10%, known subtotal .70; nested BQS uncertainty gives exact interval **2.565–8.685**, final NS. Interval endpoints are bounds, not estimates or rankings.\\n\\nG1 is not Fail and GOV is not ≤3, so the integrity cap of 3 is not activated. G3 is not categorically Fail, so its IAS cap is not activated. No discretionary override fills CAP/R/D/P. CAP is missing because a broad capital bridge is not enough to identify incremental operating returns by acquired business. R/D/P remain missing because conditional scenario prices, especially after a funding breach, are not underwritable expected returns, downside protection or valuation support. Confidence in a displayed score is distinct from its numerical value. [AR23–AR26, CALL25–26; sections 8–10 below]\\n\\n## 4. Business Overview\\n\\n**Two different businesses, plus a financial investment.** The parent develops dossiers, coordinates third-party manufacturing, registers and markets finished formulations in overseas markets. It does not earn a manufacturing moat merely by using approved contract manufacturers. Management described 35 approved manufacturers, about 20 active, with one related-group supplier and the remainder external, in the May 2026 call. Supplier substitution still requires quality, dossier and country-specific continuity. Product development, bioequivalence work, registration, pharmacovigilance, inventory, local distribution and collections remain economic obligations even without owning a large plant. [CALL26]\\n\\nThe US business sources comparator medicines used in research and bioequivalence work. This is pharmaceutical procurement and distribution, not ownership of innovator patents or a stream of patent-protected prescription royalties. FY26 RLD sales of **₹740.46 crore** represent approximately **86.7% of ₹853.63 crore group revenue**. Other disclosed revenue comprises formulations ₹93.48 crore, APIs ₹3.55 crore, retention/services ₹1.10 crore, export incentives ₹.81 crore and **advance forfeitures ₹14.23 crore**. Forfeitures must not be projected as repeat product demand. The model starts from recurring revenue of ₹839.40 crore, split into RLD ₹740.46 crore and other ₹98.94 crore. [AR26 note 30]\\n\\n**Who owns the economics:** Remus owns 56.67% of Espee Global Holdings LLC; the holding entity owns 90% of Espee Biopharma & Finechem LLC, giving **51.003% effective ownership**, not 100% and not a direct 51% shareholding. Relius entities in Bolivia and Guatemala are 99%-owned; the new Espee Clinical Trials business is 68%-owned. The clinical unit was incorporated in July 2025 and had no revenue and a small loss in FY26. The group statements consolidate 100% of subsidiary revenue before allocating profit to NCI. [AR26 AOC-1 and notes 1, 48; RESIGN24]\\n\\n**Geography and customer economics:** Group domestic/export classification was ₹325.26/₹528.37 crore in FY26. This is not a production-location breakdown. The parent alone recorded approximately ₹90.06 crore exports on ₹93.99 crore revenue. The two disclosed large customers contributed ₹132.50 crore and ₹67.13 crore. The latter amount matches related-party Rise sales, but the first customer is not identified here. No concentration-adjusted contract tenure or customer-retention cohort is available. [AR26 notes 30, 41, 45]\\n\\nManagement says RLD customers make 25–50% advances and stocks may be held until requested. That can improve funding, but only while advances, delivery acceptance and collections remain reliable. Customer advances actually rose from about ₹27.99 crore to ₹44.66 crore in FY26, while receivables and inventory rose substantially. Neither the verbal advance policy nor the reported advance liability removes the need to pay suppliers before receipt of final cash. [CALL26; AR26 notes 11, 13, 22, 24]\\n\\n## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\\n**Mechanisms that may persist:** a reusable dossier and registration organisation, long-standing local distributors, knowledge of country approvals and a supplier network can reduce launch friction. Reliable sourcing of scarce comparator drugs can attract repeat research customers. These are capabilities, not automatically exclusive rights. The dossiers must convert into approved products, commercial launches, repeat orders and collected cash; revenue growth alone does not prove durable pricing power.\\n\\n**Registration ladder, not a TAM slide:** FY25 materials referred to 620+ registered products, 400+ commercialised products and 745+ dossiers. FY26 materials referred to 903+ approvals, 1,400+ under evaluation, 2,243+ dossier registrations, 850+ ready dossiers, 430+ commercialised products and 2,100+ SKUs. These are management categories with different denominators. They are not additive unique molecules and 430/903 is not a valid approval-to-sales cohort conversion rate. No regulator-verified product-by-country list with approval date, expiry, ownership, launch sales and contribution margin was obtained. Therefore registration counts support access capability, but not a high-confidence regulatory monopoly. [DECK26, CALL25, CALL26]\\n\\nManagement reported a cleared Peru DIGEMID audit and planned filings, Saudi tender/registration efforts and semaglutide-related filings. Audit, submission, tender eligibility, approval, commercial launch and collected revenue are separate milestones. This research assigns no unearned revenue to any of those milestones. FDA guidance explicitly distinguishes registration from approval, and wholesale-distribution authorisation requires appropriate state licensure and reporting. The one exact historical Oklahoma record located for Espee Biopharma & Finechem LLC is **closed**, ending September 2022, with no disciplinary entries shown. That is **not evidence that its current US operations are unlicensed**, nor is it evidence of current authorisation elsewhere. Current jurisdiction-by-jurisdiction licences remain an open diligence item. Unrelated similarly named UK and Peruvian entities were excluded. [FDA, FDA-LICENSE, OK-LICENSE]\\n\\n**Economic comparator set, not a peer-multiple shortcut:**\\n\\n|Comparator|Relevant mechanism|Why it is not interchangeable with Remus|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|Lincoln Pharmaceuticals|Export formulations and product registration|Own manufacturing means different capital intensity, quality-control responsibilities and margins.|\\n|Marksans Pharma|Regulated-market formulations, distribution and manufacturing integration|Owned overseas/Indian manufacturing and product exposure differ from RLD comparator procurement.|\\n|Caplin Point Laboratories|Latin American front-end distribution plus formulations|Owned manufacturing, clinical capabilities and US injectable activity differ from Remus's current segment mix.|\\n|Senores Pharmaceuticals|Regulated-market manufacturing and dossiers; Remus investee|A related financial investment, not an independent valuation benchmark. Using its market multiple for Remus operations would double count the stake and obscure different ownership claims.|\\n\\nOfficial company materials support the first three structural comparisons. No comparable listed Indian pure-play RLD distributor with reconciled segment returns was established, so no fabricated peer median P/E, ROIC or market share is used. The moat score stays 5: useful access and execution capabilities, exposed to supplier concentration, regulatory interruption, customer substitution and financing capacity. [PEER-LINCOLN, PEER-MARKSANS, PEER-CAPLIN]\\n\\n## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\\n|Scope and interval|Start revenue ₹cr|End revenue ₹cr|Years|CAGR|Interpretation|\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\\n|Standalone FY20–FY26|11.9190|93.99|6|41.08%|Long-run original-business history, with accounting-basis transition; not the current group CAGR.|\\n|Standalone FY21–FY26|18.4181|93.99|5|38.54%|Useful five-year original-business scale signal, not a like-for-like consolidated cohort.|\\n|Standalone FY23–FY26|45.0273|93.99|3|27.80%|FY23 original basis and gratuity qualification disclosed.|\\n|Standalone FY24–FY26|63.94|93.99|2|21.24%|Later Ind AS comparatives; strongest recent parent comparison.|\\n|Consolidated FY24–FY26|212.94|853.63|2|100.22%|**Acquisition/full-year consolidation distortion. Not organic CAGR.**|\\n|Current consolidated group, five years|NS|NS|5|NS|No five-year same-scope history.|\\n\\nStandalone annual revenue, FY20–FY26, was ₹11.9190, 18.4181, 24.7171, 45.0273, 63.94, 79.16 and 93.99 crore. Annual additions were ₹6.4991, 6.2990, 20.3102, 18.9127, 15.22 and 14.83 crore respectively. The FY24 basis change means the ₹18.9127 crore bridge is not a pure volume/price bridge. Group additions were ₹407.42 crore in FY25 and ₹233.27 crore in FY26. [IPO, AR23–AR26]\\n\\nEspee's financial consolidation begins in FY24, with only a partial year; the definitive acquisition announcement was February 2024. It was a related-party cash acquisition, USD2.7 million, not internally generated growth. FY25 therefore captures a full-year scope step-up. By FY26, RLD revenue grew from ₹522.50 crore to ₹740.46 crore, about **41.7%**, while formulations grew from ₹69.37 crore to ₹93.48 crore, about **34.8%**. That supports existing-scope momentum, but a constant-currency price/volume/customer bridge is absent. The clinical startup had no FY26 sales; it is not the explanation for the growth. Do not classify all FY26 growth as rigorously proven organic growth. [AR24–AR26; RESIGN24]\\n\\nRecent half-years are **H1FY26 ₹400.20 crore and H2FY26 ₹453.43 crore**, versus H1FY25 ₹272.61 crore and H2FY25 ₹347.75 crore. Those halves sum to their annual totals. September 2026 earnings were not available by the cutoff, so there is no invented FY27 H1 or TTM roll-forward. Strong revenue performance coexists with a shrinking reported operating margin as low-margin RLD grows; scale is not a substitute for per-share cash generation. [FY26, H1FY26]\\n\\nREV=8 uses the documented original-business growth and recent existing-scope progress, with an explicit acquisition/history ceiling. It does **not** award a 100%-CAGR growth score to the bought revenue base.\\n\\n## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\\nThe practical scaling chain is: select product and country; secure compliant manufacturing and dossier rights; fund development and registration; win approval and distributor access; fund stock and receivables; collect cash; reinvest only the owner's share. Dossiers and SKUs are inputs to this process, not a measured addressable market. A bottom-up revenue forecast needs launches × sell-through × net realisation × retained customer share, plus repeat RLD demand. These unit cohorts are not disclosed, so scenario growth rates remain assumptions rather than an order-backed forecast.\\n\\n**Demand access versus delivery:** management targeted an increasing B2C contribution in FY25 commentary. The FY26 call reported only roughly 14–15% of non-RLD business, not of total consolidated revenue, versus a May 2025 management ambition of 25–30%; May 2026 questioners separately recalled an 18–20% target. Bolivia political/logistics delays were cited. FY26 added four named markets rather than automatically meeting an aspiration of five or more every year. Management cited 26 Bolivia launches and plans for another 40; these planned launches are not booked revenue. [CALL25, CALL26]\\n\\n**Capital is not negligible:** group receivables ₹139.84 crore plus inventory ₹72.06 crore are much larger than physical PPE. Clinical CWIP is ₹8.28 crore, with the new subsidiary financed by a parent loan around ₹9.58 crore. Continuing registrations, stock availability, export credit and supplier prepayments all consume capital. Acquiring a distribution platform can make accounting revenue rise quickly without establishing a high-return internally funded growth loop.\\n\\nThe broad invested-capital bridge in section 9 removes large financial investments and reconciles to operating capital, but there is no reliable segment/cohort bridge separating acquisition purchase price, goodwill, group transfers, operating advances, leased assets, currency translation and genuine incremental operating investment. Therefore **CAP=NS** despite apparently strong broad accounting returns. The model uses explicit growth-capital intensity, working-capital timing and minority claims; it does not reverse-engineer “asset light” into infinite growth at no cost.\\n\\nGQS remains NS, with the cash-conversion cap limiting any eventual numeric result to 5 on current evidence. To lift that cap, growth must produce sustained group cash conversion, not merely parent-only CFO or investment revaluation. Runway plausibly extends for several years, but duration is conditioned on regulatory continuity, collection performance and affordable capital, hence DUR=6.\\n\\n## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\\n**Audit history is not uniformly clean.** The IPO restatement report, printed F2–F3, expressly records qualified original FY20, FY21 and FY22 opinions because long-term employee benefits had been accounted for on a cash basis rather than accrued. Annexure 29 incorporates retrospective actuarial adjustments into the restated numbers. Printed F4 expressly distinguishes the restatement exercise from reissuing or redating the original opinions. Those old original audit reports were not separately obtained; the qualification history is supported firsthand by the signed restatement report, not inferred. FY23's audit was genuinely qualified on gratuity accounting: current-year expense of ₹10.22 lakh and an opening reserve adjustment of ₹19.51 lakh were at issue. The IPO restatement and later Ind AS statements are different vintages; their adjustments do not erase the original opinion. FY24's main opinion reads unmodified, while its basis paragraph refers to a qualified opinion and its other-matter paragraph acknowledges the prior modified opinion. This is an internal wording inconsistency, not a licence to label the whole history clean or to allege fraud. [AR23, AR24, IPO]\\n\\nParikh & Majmudar resigned effective 6 February 2024; Pankaj R. Shah and Associates succeeded them. The signed resignation letter and annexure state the reason as avoiding a potential future conflict of interest, with information-withholding concerns marked not applicable. The evidence does **not** support an allegation that the resignation resulted from obstruction or undisclosed wrongdoing. FY26's current audit opinion is unmodified, with revenue recognition a key audit matter; the standalone CARO and internal-financial-control reports do not report fraud/default or ineffective controls. These are positive assurance facts but do not guarantee recoverability of every balance. [RESIGN24; AR26 audit and annexures]\\n\\n**Assurance perimeter:** two small foreign subsidiaries were included using management-certified figures, approximately assets ₹11.80 crore, revenue ₹9.34 crore, PAT ₹3.06 crore and cash outflow ₹.15 crore. Two other subsidiaries were audited by other auditors, approximately revenue ₹755.79 crore and PAT ₹19.27 crore. The main consolidated report relies on those auditors for that work. Note 55 also states that confirmations of supplier, customer, loan and advance balances were awaited at approval, with book balances used. This is a material diligence limitation, not proof of fictitious balances. [AR26 consolidated audit, note 55]\\n\\n**Minority and earnings reconciliation:** main FY26 P&L total PAT ₹46.17 crore splits into owners ₹36.99 crore and NCI ₹9.18 crore. Reported EPS ₹39.19 corresponds to total PAT, whereas owner EPS on current shares is **₹31.39**. FY25 has the same issue: total PAT ₹38.42 crore versus owners ₹29.07 crore. Note 48 and AOC-1 show subsidiary profit/NCI figures that do not cleanly reconcile to the main allocation; for example, Espee profit ₹13.24 crore/NCI ₹6.49 crore differs from the main NCI and external-auditor perimeter. Do not calibrate a US segment margin from whichever inconsistent number gives the best valuation. Use main owner PAT for observed P/E and disclosed economic ownership only as an explicit forward proxy. [AR26 pp138–141, AOC-1, notes 48–49]\\n\\n**Related parties and allocation:** FY26 Rise sales ₹67.13 crore and purchases ₹10.19 crore sit within the separately disclosed ₹100 crore transaction ceiling; this report does not allege a cap breach. Rise receivables fell from ₹25.51 crore to ₹7.58 crore, which is a positive movement but not proof of collection of every outstanding related balance. Other items include Ratnatris purchases ₹8.35 crore, Senores development ₹2.14 crore, Renosen consultancy ₹7.37 crore, Espee Therapeutics ₹3.58 crore and Shashin Shah consultancy ₹4.41 crore. KMP remuneration and other benefits are separately disclosed. The operating rationale, pricing, recoverability and allocation to listed shareholders warrant continued review. Detailed related-party loan totals and presentation categories also need reconciliation rather than casual addition. [AR26 note 41; BALLOT25]\\n\\n**Capital allocation:** the Ratnatris stake exchange produced 3,261,744 Senores shares. The investment is carried through OCI, not operating PAT. A large rise in equity from investment marking is not evidence of cash reinvestment returns. IPO proceeds of ₹47.69 crore were reported fully used by March 2025, including working capital ₹30.31 crore, acquisitions/general purposes ₹16.38 crore and issue costs ₹1 crore. Reported utilisation is not proof that every resulting investment has met its hurdle. Historical guarantee/security exposure, about ₹75.35 crore in FY24, is not carried forward as a current obligation because later FY25 disclosures state nil and FY26 does not repeat it. However, exact current release/encumbrance evidence remains necessary before treating the listed stake as freely available cash. [AR24–AR26]\\n\\n|Dated statement or decision|Observed result by cutoff|Assessment|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|February 2024 Espee acquisition, USD2.7m, related-party|RLD became the dominant revenue stream; partial-year to full-year consolidation materially changed scale|Growth achieved, but compare owner cash and acquisition returns, not just sales.|\\n|May 2025 B2C ambition 25–30%; May 2026 questioners recalled 18–20%|FY26 call about 14–15% of non-RLD revenue|Below prior ambition; denominator must not become total group revenue.|\\n|Pipeline registrations and country expansion|Larger claimed pipeline; four named new markets in FY26; regulator-level conversion register unavailable|Partial operating progress, not all milestones independently verified.|\\n|FY26 clinical research expansion|CWIP and loans increased, startup remained pre-revenue|Capital deployed ahead of economic proof.|\\n|September 2026 main-board migration proposal|Shareholder approval reported 7 October; 19 October master still active SM|Approval is not completion and does not remove SME liquidity risk.|\\n\\nGOV=5 and EXEC=5 balance real growth and current assurance against these unresolved accounting, related-party and delivery issues. The May 2026 presentation reports March 31 ownership of promoters 71.0%, foreign portfolio investors 5.3%, alternative investment funds .8% and other public 22.9%. The April 2, 2026 promoter declaration reports nil encumbrance at March 31 and no encumbrance created in FY26; this is historical, not a current-date zero-pledge assurance. Latest promoter changes and insider transactions still require a cutoff-matched exchange update. The FY26 board report also records Anjali Shah’s CFO departure effective July 8, 2026 and Bhavik Shah’s appointment from July 9, approved June 23. No inference of wrongdoing is drawn from that transition. [AR26 board report; DECK26; PLEDGE26]\\n\\n## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\\nAll figures below are ₹ crore; consolidated FY24 is the later comparable Ind AS vintage.\\n\\n|Year|Revenue|Owner PAT|Total PAT|CFO|Reported capex|FCF|Recurring EBIT|Broad IC|\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n|FY24|212.94|20.52|24.28|1.35|3.29|-1.94|26.07|39.43|\\n|FY25|620.36|29.07|38.42|6.98|3.98|3.00|34.73|62.00|\\n|FY26|853.63|36.99|46.17|18.16|14.57|3.59|39.71|93.21|\\n\\n**Parent-only history, kept separate from the acquired consolidated group:**\\n\\n|Standalone year / basis|Revenue|PAT|CFO|Reported capex|FCF proxy|Borrowings|Cash as reported|\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n|FY20 / Indian GAAP, IPO restated|11.9190|0.8116|1.3026|0.5592|0.7434|0.3593|1.1981|\\n|FY21 / Indian GAAP, IPO restated|18.4181|1.0258|2.8379|0.0664|2.7715|0.2789|0.8305|\\n|FY22 / Indian GAAP, IPO restated|24.7171|3.3857|5.0378|0.0710|4.9668|0.1917|1.1486|\\n|FY23 / Indian GAAP; gratuity-qualified original; not silently restated|45.0273|8.5028|5.3140|2.7316|2.5824|7.9500|3.0382|\\n|FY24 / Ind AS FY25 comparative restatement|63.9400|16.6200|6.3100|3.5900|2.7200|1.4400|1.7100|\\n|FY25 / Ind AS|79.1600|19.9000|15.5900|3.9500|11.6400|2.1400|3.4600|\\n|FY26 / Ind AS|93.9900|24.7100|25.5000|3.9200|21.5800|4.3300|2.7600|\\n\\nThe FY20–FY22 figures are IPO-restated observations, not evidence that the original audits were unqualified. FY23 is the original Indian-GAAP vintage, including cash/bank deposits in its reported cash definition; the later transition comparison separates approximately ₹1.01 crore cash equivalents and ₹2.03 crore other bank balances. It is not an unrestricted-cash series. FY24–FY26 use the stated Ind AS scope. Original and restated vintages remain separately identifiable in the inputs; none is spliced into a current-group five-year CAGR. Calculated trading gross profit and EBITDA, including recurring versions that remove forfeitures and non-operating income where appropriate, are retained in historical_metrics.json rather than represented as company-reported segment margins.\\n\\n**Revenue → earnings → cash.** FY26 PBT ₹59.16 crore plus finance ₹1.47 crore gives reported EBIT ₹60.63 crore. Remove other income ₹6.69 crore and advance forfeitures ₹14.23 crore to obtain **₹39.71 crore recurring EBIT**, approximately 4.73% of recurring revenue ₹839.40 crore. This is a research normalization, not a company-reported segment result. Owner PAT is not obtained simply by applying a generic 49% deduction to all earnings. The historical owner-core-PAT diagnostic removes 75% of forfeitures and other income from owner PAT, but it is deliberately not used as a definitive owner profit forecast because entity allocation is unresolved.\\n\\nFY26 cash flow starts with PBT ₹59.16 crore, reaches approximately ₹61.30 crore before working-capital movements after depreciation, interest, finance and other adjustments, and then absorbs approximately ₹31.81 crore into receivables and ₹20.50 crore into inventory. Payables contributed ₹4.76 crore and other liabilities ₹17.32 crore; other asset/liability movements complete reported cash generated ₹27.62 crore. Tax paid ₹9.46 crore leaves **CFO ₹18.16 crore**. Cash investing outflow was ₹15.03 crore and financing inflow ₹1.47 crore; net change ₹4.59 crore reconciles ₹7.23 crore opening to ₹11.82 crore closing cash. Component rounding is retained rather than forced into a fictitious adjustment. [AR26 cash-flow statement and notes]\\n\\nGroup CFO/PAT is approximately **24.33% over FY24–FY26**, with total CFO ₹26.49 crore against total PAT ₹108.87 crore. Aggregate reported capex was ₹21.84 crore and FCF ₹4.65 crore. Parent standalone CFO improved to ₹25.50 crore in FY26 against PAT ₹24.71 crore, but parent cash performance cannot replace group cash generation when most consolidated revenue and a large NCI claim sit elsewhere. CASH=3 reflects persistent group conversion weakness, not a claim that every subsidiary is cash-loss-making.\\n\\n**Lease presentation, preserved and normalized separately:** reported FY26 capex/cash purchase line ₹14.57 crore includes leasehold assets, while financing includes a positive lease-liability movement about ₹1.85 crore. Note 21 shows non-cash lease additions ₹2.57 crore, cash rental ₹.95 crore and interest ₹.24 crore. Removing the non-cash addition from both sides suggests cash capex around **₹12.00 crore** and lease principal around **₹.71 crore**, within rounding, rather than genuine cash funding of ₹1.85 crore. Reported FCF remains ₹3.59 crore; diagnostic capex-adjusted FCF is about ₹6.16 crore **before separately classifying lease principal**, not a second free-cash benefit. Gross ROU depreciation ₹.86 crore less ₹.19 crore capitalised explains net ₹.67 crore; gross total depreciation ₹2.94 crore versus P&L ₹2.76 crore is substantially explained by that capitalization and rounding. It is not left as an unexplained audit discrepancy. [AR26 notes 5, 21, 36]\\n\\n**Working capital and capital returns:** broad invested capital is defined as owner equity + NCI + borrowings + lease liabilities − cash − other bank balances − deposits − financial investments − loans. This gives approximately **₹39.43 crore, ₹62.00 crore and ₹93.21 crore** in FY24, FY25 and FY26. It includes goodwill and operating leased assets, and uses carrying values. Recurring EBIT ×75% is a standardized NOPAT proxy; dividing by average broad IC gives roughly 51% in FY25 and 38% in FY26. The incremental NOPAT/ΔIC proxy is much lower and volatile, roughly 29% then 12%. The operating side is net operating working capital plus PPE/intangibles, CWIP, intangible development, goodwill and ROU, plus other non-current operating assets: ₹53.25/₹89.33/₹135.63 crore. Financing minus operating capital is −₹13.82/−₹27.33/−₹42.42 crore, fully reconciled at disclosed rounding to non-deposit financial assets less non-advance current liabilities, provisions and current/deferred tax. The arithmetic residual is zero, but allocating deferred tax between financial investments and operations is not solved by that identity. Average operating ROIC proxies are 36.54%/26.48% for FY25/FY26, materially below the financial-asset-stripped broad ratios. Exact calculations, average owner ROE and total-capital ROCE are retained in historical_metrics.json. Those figures are **not verified segment/cohort returns**: acquisition scope, non-cash adjustments, investment stripping, OCI tax and customer-advance effects matter. They support further diligence, not a numeric CAP score.\\n\\nReceivable/revenue days at year-end are about 198, 64 and 60 for FY24–FY26; the FY24 denominator contains only a partial US year, so its apparent improvement is materially distorted. Inventory/revenue and payable/revenue days are disclosed as proxies only. Separately, reported purchases plus inventory-expense change gives trading COGS ₹163.37/₹532.87/₹742.21 crore; the calculation files include closing and average-inventory COGS days and average-payables/purchase days, with the partial-year FY24 limitation retained. FY26 receivables ₹139.84 crore plus stock ₹72.06 crore, less payables ₹76.04 crore and customer advances ₹44.66 crore, leave about **₹91.20 crore** before other operating balances. Even a modest collection delay can overwhelm cash ₹11.82 crore.\\n\\n**Liquidity, debt and assets:** FY26 borrowings ₹23.04 crore comprise ₹17.37 crore due within a year and ₹5.67 crore later; demand working-capital borrowing is approximately ₹17.04 crore. Leases total ₹4.03 crore, including ₹1.01 crore current. The maturity table divides borrowings ₹17.37/₹4.64/₹1.03 crore and leases ₹1.01/₹2.67/₹.35 crore across the disclosed buckets. Current deposits ₹24.50 crore are the current portion of financial-institution deposits with original maturity over 12 months, **not cash equivalents**. Other bank balances ₹1.06 crore, loans ₹9.65 crore, mutual funds ₹10.32 crore and AIF ₹7.21 crore are classified separately. Remaining disclosed capital commitments are about ₹.57 crore. No current credit rating or unused committed line was independently established. [AR26 notes 9, 12, 14, 20, 21, 42, 45]\\n\\n**Senores investment:** 3,261,744 shares cost about ₹20.55 crore and were marked at ₹257.03 crore at March 2026. Total FY26 financial investments were ₹274.56 crore. The October 19 official Senores close ₹1,253.60 implies **₹408.89 crore** for the disclosed share count, subject to no subsequent stake change. The holding quantity after March and current encumbrance are not proven by a price quote. FY26 OCI included a substantial fair-value gain; approximately ₹69.70 crore gross investment uplift and ₹60.15 crore total OCI explain much of equity growth without funding operations. The model neither treats OCI as EBIT nor adds both the stake value and capitalised investment earnings.\\n\\n**Per-share and foreign-currency discipline:** July 2025 1:1 and the earlier 3:1 bonus changed denominators, not economic ownership. Current issued shares are 11,784,000; authorised capital corresponds to 12,500,000 shares, leaving a modeled hard ceiling of 716,000 additional shares unless a future authorization occurs. No prospective authorization is assumed. Currency exposure notes disclose a net USD exposure equivalent to ₹15.91 crore and euro exposure around ₹.39 crore, but this is not a complete hedge or gross US-revenue exposure analysis. Translation, pricing currency, procurement currency and blocked/repatriable cash are different risks; the model stresses margins and available cash rather than assuming a fully natural hedge. [AR26 capital and risk notes; MASTER]\\n\\n## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\\n**Observed entry metrics:** market cap ₹1160.13 crore; corrected owner EPS ₹31.39; owner P/E **31.36x**. The lower **25.13x** total-PAT multiple is not the shareholder's earnings multiple. Neither a group EV/EBITDA multiple that ignores NCI nor a P/E after subtracting the entire unencumbered stake at spot is used as a purchase rule. The financial holding and two operating engines require an explicit sum-of-parts bridge.\\n\\n### SME execution reality\\n\\nThe 60 official sessions from 24 July through 19 October show mean daily traded value ₹1,02,58,814 and median ₹50,73,552.50. The last 20 sessions show mean ₹94,15,796.50 and median ₹39,31,495, with no missing or zero-turnover observations. At the lower of the 60-day mean and median, a ₹5 lakh exit needs theoretical 4.9275 / 1.9710 / .9855 sessions at 2% / 5% / 10% participation. Whole-lot capacity uses each historical session's actual traded **shares**, not rupees divided by today's price: at least one 100-share lot fits on 34 / 57 / 60 of the 60 sessions respectively. At entry, one lot costs ₹98,450, five lots ₹4,92,250 and six ₹5,90,700. Fractions of a session, historical turnover and a quoted close do not guarantee a fill. No position-size or exit promise is inferred from this reference.\\n\\nThe framework starts at L=9 for the theoretical 1.971-day figure. The explicit judgment adjustment is **9→7**, reflecting observed concentration (the top five sessions supply 41.85% of 60-day turnover), the lower recent 20-day median and indivisible lots. Median 5%-participation capacity is two lots. Completed rolling exits take two sessions median for five lots and three for six, with maxima five/six and two right-censored end-windows. Unknown spreads/circuits are limitations, not observed adverse evidence. [TAPE, MASTER]\\n\\n### Cash-linked scenario construction\\n\\nThe executable inputs start with audited **31 March 2026 cash ₹11.82 crore**, borrowings ₹23.04 crore and disclosed working-capital balances. Seven pre-entry periods total **202 days** through October 19. Those are projected movements, not unaudited actual cash. No pre-entry refinancing, stock sale or issue is permitted; current issued shares must reconcile to the exchange master. The announced dividend is deducted in the pre-entry bridge. After entry, 60 monthly periods give five years, followed by 12 continuation months to test terminal operating feasibility.\\n\\nEach period records revenue, cash operating cost, target receivables/inventory/payables/advances, gradual balance transition, collections, supplier cash, tax, interest, cash leases, capex, principal and shares. Supplier cash is paid **before** receipts within each cash cycle; period-end cash alone is insufficient. The main case uses **8 cycles per month**, with 1/2/4/8/12/16/24/32 alternatives. This is an explicit timing assumption, not measured supplier credit. The frequency16 illustration is shown separately because changing cash timing materially changes feasibility; it does not replace or conceal the failed main case.\\n\\nOperating margins are before cash lease rent but after physical depreciation. Physical depreciation is .249% of revenue, based on approximate net non-ROU depreciation, and cash rent ₹.95 crore annually is deducted once. Tax is 25% of positive earnings after cash rent and interest, an assumption rather than entity-specific tax advice. Maintenance capex is .4% of sales; growth fixed investment is 3% of the **rolling prior-year revenue increment**; clinical completion spending is separately modeled. Working-capital balances move gradually toward scenario days/advance ratios. Demand debt is repaid in modeled post-entry month five, approximately March 2027, rather than silently rolled over, and other principal is amortised. New debt incurs interest beginning the following monthly period; higher-rate sensitivities expose that convention rather than treating it as free finance.\\n\\n|Assumption|Bear|Base|Bull|\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n|RLD annual growth|2%|12%|20%|\\n|Other recurring revenue growth|8%|18%|25%|\\n|RLD EBIT margin before cash rent|1.8%|2.5%|3.2%|\\n|Other EBIT margin before cash rent|18%|21.5%|27%|\\n|Receivable days target|85|60|50|\\n|Inventory / payable days target|45 / 30|34 / 34|28 / 36|\\n|Customer-advance / annual sales target|3.5%|5.3%|6.5%|\\n|Senores price growth / terminal haircut|−3% / 35%|6% / 25%|10% / 15%|\\n|Additional currency margin drag|.3 percentage points|0|0|\\n\\n**Financing modes are separate:** no-new-finance permits no refinancing, equity or discretionary asset monetisation. Deposit maturities are not treated as committed because exact dates are absent. Bounded-optional permits, only after entry, finite mutual-fund/deposit sales with a 1% realization haircut, then up to ₹15 crore incremental bank debt, then a **whole-share issue**, priced at 25% below entry with 3% issue cost. New issue count cannot exceed 716,000, authorised headroom, or ₹50 crore net cash raised. These are alternative possibilities, **not approved or committed funding**. Unspent investments are retained in terminal value; sold amounts cannot be counted again. No Senores sale funds a rescue. A dedicated stress removes liquid financial assets and new debt so that the whole-share mechanism is tested at zero, 100,000 and 716,000-share caps.\\n\\n**First breach is absorbing:** every pre-entry, intra-period, annual and terminal-proof cash event is tested against a ₹2 crore minimum. Once breached, ordinary CAGR and terminal equity return become **null/NS**. The engine retains the unpaid/failing path and shortfall but never manufactures a normal return on an unfunded operating plan. A terminal-proof failure also removes the ordinary return. A scenario breach is not proof that the real issuer ran out of cash; it demonstrates missing timing/funding evidence.\\n\\n|Scenario / finance|Status|First breach|Conditional five-year CAGR|Issued shares|\\n|---|---|---|---:|---:|\\n|bear/no_new_finance|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre1|NS|0|\\n|bear/bounded_optional|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre1|NS|0|\\n|base/no_new_finance|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre2|NS|0|\\n|base/bounded_optional|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre2|NS|0|\\n|bull/no_new_finance|CONDITIONAL|None|5.61%|0|\\n|bull/bounded_optional|CONDITIONAL|None|5.61%|0|\\n|bear/frequency16/no_new_finance|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre1|NS|0|\\n|bear/frequency16/bounded_optional|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre1|NS|0|\\n|base/frequency16/no_new_finance|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|investment/Y1M06|NS|0|\\n|base/frequency16/bounded_optional|CONDITIONAL|None|-4.57%|0|\\n|bull/frequency16/no_new_finance|CONDITIONAL|None|5.70%|0|\\n|bull/frequency16/bounded_optional|CONDITIONAL|None|5.70%|0|\\n\\n### Earned terminal valuation, not assumed rerating\\n\\nTerminal year-six revenue grows at 4%; operating discount/capitalisation rates are 14% RLD and 13% other, stressed separately. FCFF is derived from the **same 12 continuation rows** used for the cash test: after-cash-rent unlevered NOPAT + physical depreciation − actual continuation capex − actual change in operating working capital. Shared capital/cash flows are allocated by revenue explicitly because segment cash data are absent. No positive FCFF or no valid k−g means no supported terminal. A cash reserve equal to the continuation path's maximum cumulative draw plus minimum cash is retained; **only cash above that reserve** is added to equity. Debt is deducted, economic minority claims are applied to segment equity, and lease debt is not deducted again after rent has already been charged.\\n\\nRLD economic NCI is 48.997%, other-business NCI proxy 1%, both assumptions tied to the ownership chain and tested. The cash/debt allocation of 80% to RLD is also sensitivity-tested. This is not a claim that all subsidiaries can freely upstream cash. Add only the separately valued Senores holding after the stated haircut and 15% gain-tax reserve, plus unspent funds/deposits and explicitly impaired loan/AIF recoveries. The tax reserve is a conservative scenario assumption, not a determination of actual tax treatment. Future dividends are zero; all return measures are five-year price CAGR after modeled dilution.\\n\\n### Reverse expectations and sensitivities\\n\\nThe margin reverse test searches RLD EBIT margins **0–30%** with other base assumptions and frequency16 held fixed. Every scan and bisection trial is retained. It only bisects adjacent feasible sign-changing endpoints; any failed funding/terminal midpoint invalidates that bracket. No root is inferred across a cash-failure discontinuity. Bisection brackets and residuals are in reverse_results.json.\\n\\n|Target CAGR|Finance|RLD EBIT-margin root bracket|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|12.00%|no_new_finance|16.933938% to 16.933938%|\\n|12.00%|bounded_optional|16.933938% to 16.933938%|\\n|15.00%|no_new_finance|20.638201% to 20.638201%|\\n|15.00%|bounded_optional|20.638201% to 20.638201%|\\n|18.00%|no_new_finance|24.749833% to 24.749833%|\\n|18.00%|bounded_optional|24.749833% to 24.749833%|\\n\\nA separate revenue-growth scan covers **0–50% RLD growth** at fixed 2.5% RLD margin, without claiming continuity or an exact root across financing boundaries. The hurdle prices at 12%, 15% and 18% are computed exactly from ₹984.50×(1+hurdle)^5. These tests expose how much margin, growth and funding the entry price demands; they do not establish that the company can achieve it.\\n\\nOne-way tests cover opening available cash, collection days, inventory days, advances, cycle frequency, debt caps/rates, NCI, allocation of debt/cash, Senores growth/haircut, terminal growth and capitalisation rates, FX margin drag and rollover assumptions. Two-way tests cover RLD growth × margin and receivable days × terminal capitalisation rate. The whole-share rescue tests include insufficient-cap cases. All 312 unique paths and 502 aliases, including failed roots and duplicate evaluations, are preserved losslessly; paths.json.gz is a deduplicated path pool, not a summary that discards failures.\\n\\n**No supportable buy range is asserted.** A low model price in a failed path is not a bargain price, and a conditional positive return cannot overcome unresolved cash availability, minority and accounting gates. Any future entry range must be rebuilt with verified current cash, liabilities, contractual financing, ownership and a defensible capital allocation, then clear the hurdle without an unearned terminal multiple.\\n\\n## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\\nThe strongest bear case is not that revenue stops growing. It is that **thin-margin RLD growth consumes cash faster than suppliers and banks will fund it**, while the listed shareholder owns only about half of the US engine. Add delayed collection, slower inventory turnover, reduced customer advances and adverse currency economics. Cash fails before annual PAT reveals the problem; optional financing may be unavailable or dilutive; registration/clinical spending then competes with working capital. The model's early bear/base timing failures illustrate this mechanism without asserting an observed default.\\n\\nA second loss channel is investment concentration. A lower Senores price, a higher realization haircut, encumbrance or non-transferable ownership reduces the financial cushion. It is not appropriate to use a full spot stake value as immediate debt-repayment cash while also capitalising its future upside. A third is regulatory interruption at a CMO or distributor, causing approval delays, recalls, unusable inventory or loss of comparator supply. A fourth is poor related-party capital allocation that leaves listed shareholders with cash claims rather than distributable cash.\\n\\nPermanent impairment can arise from dilutive rescue, receivable write-offs, expired inventory, lost licences or an acquisition price/operating-cost structure that cannot earn its capital cost. A price drawdown alone is not permanent impairment; conversely, a large balance-sheet investment does not prevent operating liquidity failure. The **thesis-break variable is verified group cash generation available after minority claims and obligatory capital needs**, not a single revenue growth rate. No precise recovery value or downside score is fabricated for failed paths. D stays NS.\\n\\n## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\\nA five-year **5×** price requires approximately **37.97% annual appreciation** and a price of **₹4,922.50**; **10×** requires approximately **58.49%** and **₹9,845.00**. At unchanged shares those imply market caps of about ₹5,800.67 crore and ₹11,601.35 crore. At a hypothetical 20× owner P/E, required owner PAT would be about ₹290.03 crore and ₹580.07 crore; at 30×, ₹193.36 crore and ₹386.71 crore. These are algebraic hurdles, not target multiples.\\n\\nAgainst FY26 owner PAT ₹36.99 crore, the required earnings growth is very substantial, before accounting for dilution, minority claims and capital funding. Group revenue growing rapidly at a 2–3% RLD operating margin does not automatically create those owner earnings. A multi-bagger case must demonstrate expansion of higher-margin formulations or durable improvement in RLD economics, repeatable cash conversion, sufficient capital and a supportable terminal value. The current dossier and commercialisation counts do not establish this. No 5×/10× probability is assigned, and no acquired-revenue CAGR is extrapolated to manufacture one.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\\n|KPI and baseline|Evidence needed / next milestone|Warning or thesis-break threshold|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|Owner EPS ₹31.39 versus reported ₹39.19 FY26|Company/auditor reconciliation of EPS numerator, bonus denominator, AOC-1 and Note48|Unresolved after next audited cycle, or further inconsistencies: keep G2 Hold.|\\n|Group CFO/PAT 24.3% over three years|Next two reporting periods and rolling three-year bridge; reconcile parent/subsidiary cash|Rolling conversion below50% while high growth persists is a warning; negative available cash without committed funding breaks investability.|\\n|Receivables ₹139.84cr and inventory ₹72.06cr|Ageing, subsequent receipts, inventory expiry and purchase obligations by entity|DSO >75 or material >180-day receivables without recoverability evidence; investigate rather than mechanically accept sales.|\\n|Advances ₹44.66cr; forfeiture income ₹14.23cr|Separate recurring sales from releases of historical advances|Profit growth dependent on forfeitures while customer advances decline undermines earnings quality.|\\n|Demand borrowing about ₹17.04cr|Renewal letters, covenants, unused committed lines and currency/entity restrictions|Renewal absent near maturity or inability to maintain a reasonable intraperiod cash buffer.|\\n|RLD ₹740.46cr; effective parent interest51.003%|Audited segment cash margin and shareholder-distribution reconciliation|Top-line growth without incremental owner cash; minority leakage larger than modeled.|\\n|903+ approvals /430+ commercialised, management categories|Product-country register, regulator status, launch dates and sales by cohort|Expiry, rejection or commercialisation slippage; no automatic credit for filing counts.|\\n|B2C about14–15% of non-RLD revenue|Like-for-like FY27 share, margins, cash costs and country milestones|Another miss against own targets without denominator-consistent explanation.|\\n|Senores3,261,744shares, Marchholding|Latest ownership/encumbrance and investment-allocation policy|Stake unavailable for holders, loss beyond haircut assumptions, or new related-party deployment without clear returns.|\\n|SMlot100; median60-session turnover₹50.74lakh|Updated official tape, spread/depth/circuit/series facts before action|Entry/exit not feasible within conservative participation; migration proposal alone does not cure it.|\\n\\nThese are research triggers, not a commitment to trade automatically. Update after the next financial release, material acquisition/financing, auditor clarification or listing-series change, using a new explicit cutoff.\\n\\n## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\\n**Classification: Hold–unresolved; not ranked.** BQS/GQS/IAS remain NS. This is not a “high-quality but expensive” label with a hidden numeric score; evidence gaps prevent that classification. It is also not a proven fraud, default or worthless business. The original business grew substantially, current audits contain meaningful assurance, and the investment stake is economically material. Those positives coexist with weak group conversion, minority-heavy distribution earnings and unresolved accounting/financing detail.\\n\\nThe compounder label is withheld because durable high incremental returns, self-funded growth, clean owner earnings and adequate entry return have not all been demonstrated. No buy/avoid trade instruction or portfolio weight is produced from the present conditional models. Price matters, but a lower quote alone cannot fix unclear cash claims. Reconsider after (1) reconciled EPS/subsidiary allocations, (2) verified legal-entity cash and contractual finance, (3) cash-converting product/customer cohorts, and (4) a cash-feasible owner return comfortably above the hurdle at a new price. Until then, the action is diligence/watchlist rather than ranking with fully underwritten companies.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\\n### Source ledger\\n\\nSource IDs used above map to official documents and exact reviewed sections in source_register.json. Annual-report printed pages are used where available; the FY26 PDF contains facing-page spreads, so PDF page number and printed page differ. The working papers retain numerical transcriptions and public-source references, not a republished annual report.\\n\\n- **AR26:** [FY2025–26 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Annual-Report-FY-2025-2026.pdf), September 2026; standalone audit/CARO/IFC, consolidated opinion/other matters, full statements and notes, especially 5, 9–24, 30, 36, 41–45, 48–55. Main group statements printed 138–141; subsidiary and allocation tables separately checked.\\n- **AR25:** [FY2024–25 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Annual-Report-FY-2024-2025.pdf), September 2025; both audit reports/annexures, statements, restatement/transition, acquisition, investments, related parties, IPO use and guarantee disclosures.\\n- **AR24:** [FY2023–24 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual-Report-FY-2023-2024.pdf), August 2024; opinion wording, CARO/resignation, financials, acquisition and guarantee schedules. Original vintage retained separately rather than mixed into later comparatives.\\n- **AR23:** [FY2022–23 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/Annual-Report-2022-2023.pdf), August 2023; original qualified audit and gratuity treatment, statements and notes.\\n- **IPO:** [Final prospectus](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/Prospectus.pdf), May 2023; identity, capital, risk factors, business, objects, restatement report and financial annexures. Old history is not the present consolidated group.\\n- **FY26:** [May 2026 audited results](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/FINANCIAL-RESULTS-FOR-HALF-YEAR-AND-YEAR-ENDED-MARCH-31-2026.pdf), 20 May 2026; both audit opinions, annual and half-year comparisons.\\n- **H1FY26:** [September 2025 half-year results](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/UNAUDITED-FINANCIAL-RESULTS-FOR-HALF-YEAR-ENDED-30.09.2025.pdf), 10 November 2025; limited-review period only, not September 2026.\\n- **CALL25 / CALL26:** [FY25 call](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Investors-Conference-Call-Transcript-H2-FY25.pdf) and [FY26 call](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Investors-Conference-Call-Transcript-H2-FY26-FY26.pdf), May 2025/May 2026; management claims, targets and Q&A distinguished from audited facts.\\n- **DECK26:** [FY26 investor presentation](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Investor-Presentation-H2-FY26-FY26.pdf), May 2026; claimed registration/launch categories, not independent regulatory certifications.\\n- **RESIGN24:** [7 February 2024 board outcome and annexures](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/REMUS_07022024180724_Outcomeofboardmeetingsigned.pdf); acquisition price/related-party terms and signed auditor resignation reason.\\n- **MIGRATION26:** [7 October 2026 shareholder approval](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/REMUS_07102026191521_OutcomeofPostalBallotsigned.pdf); special resolution passed, not an exchange-effective migration.\\n- **BALLOT25 / BALLOT26:** [Rise transaction notice](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Notice-of-Postal-Ballot-10.11.2025.pdf) and [migration proposal](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Notice-of-Postal-Ballot-02.09.2026.pdf); ceilings/proposals do not prove completion or funding.\\n- **PLEDGE26:** [Promoter encumbrance declaration](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/team_bbodade_09042026155545_REMUS.pdf),2 April 2026, available 9 April 2026; nil atMarch 31 only.\\n- **MASTER / TAPE:** [NSE security master, 19 October 2026](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz) and [official daily bhavcopy](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/BhavCopy_NSE_CM_0_0_0_20261019_F_0000.csv.zip). market_extract.json contains all 60 exact-issuer observations, original URLs and archive/content hashes, including day counts; investment_market_extract.json separately records the Senores quote.\\n- **FDA / FDA-LICENSE:** [Registration versus approval](https://www.fda.gov/consumers/consumer-updates/it-really-fda-approved) and [wholesale-distributor licence checking](https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-supply-chain-integrity/check-licensure-wholesale-drug-distributors-and-third-party-logistics-providers), 2026 guidance available before cutoff. Used for terminology, not an assertion of issuer approval.\\n- **OK-LICENSE:** [Exact historical Oklahoma Espee record](https://lv.pharmacy.ok.gov/FacilityDetail.asp?CountyTypeLic=88-W-4787), update dated 11 September 2026; closed historical licence, not a nationwide conclusion.\\n- **PEER-LINCOLN / PEER-MARKSANS / PEER-CAPLIN:** [Lincoln FY25 report](https://www.lincolnpharma.com/Investor/Disclosures%20under%20Regulation%2046%20of%20the%20LODR/15.%20Annual%20Reports/2024-25/Annual%20Report%20F_2024-25%20LPL.pdf), [Marksans FY25 report](https://www.marksanspharma.com/pdf/annual-report/marksans-annual-report-2024-2025.pdf), [Caplin structural business description](https://www.caplinpoint.net/index.php/our-business/). Mechanism comparisons only; no subsequent financial result or spot peer multiple imported.\\n\\n### Reconstructibility and model limits\\n\\nThe authored inputs are historical_inputs.json, model_inputs.json, score_inputs.json, source_register.json, framework_provenance.json, market_extract.json, security_extract.json, investment_market_extract.json and this report template. Standard-library build.py/model_engine.py/path_codec.py regenerate all research tables, exact scores, liquidity, full cash-event paths, aliases, sensitivities and reverse trials. verify.py independently checks arithmetic, ledger identities, share caps, absorbing breaches, terminal cash/capital alignment and reconstruction. guarded_rebuild.py removes generated outputs and old attestations before rebuilding with an explicit read/write allowlist and denied network/subprocess calls. The compact recovery archive contains every minimal input and executable dependency; the full archive adds generated outputs. Neither requires the original source PDFs, OCR, a helper installation, network access or a private path to recreate the research calculations.\\n\\n**Material assumptions are not facts:** cash pooling; supplier-before-collection frequency; working-capital target adjustment; growth/margin/tax; useful life/maintenance/growth capex; debt maturity timing and uncommitted alternatives; issue price/fees/headroom; minority allocation; terminal capitalisation rates and continuity; available investment holdings, market growth, realization haircut/tax and residual loan recovery. The scenario engine makes these falsifiable, not known. Source-safe extracts reproduce the entered numbers and provenance, but cannot recreate the issuer's undisclosed contracts or prove future cash.\\n\\n**Open questions in priority order:** (1) corrected EPS and subsidiary allocation reconciliation; (2) current cash, restrictions, balances and bank renewal by entity; (3) customer/supplier confirmations and subsequent collections; (4) clean non-cash lease cash-flow reconciliation; (5) product-country regulatory register and commercialisation economics; (6) segment/cohort incremental capital and owner distributions; (7) current Senores quantity, encumbrance and capital-allocation policy; (8) recent shareholding/pledges and live execution constraints. These gaps are carried as NS or conditional status, not quietly converted into favourable assumptions.\\n\\nResearch update: initial v3 dossier with cutoff 20 October 2026. No trade is implied. Changes to sources, inputs, scoring or models require a new deterministic rebuild and independent review.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive Verdict\"","\"\\n**HOLD–UNRESOLVED. Not ranked; not an underwritten compounder.** Remus combines an outsourced-formulation export and registration business with a much larger, thin-margin US reference-listed-drug (RLD) distribution business acquired from promoter-related entities. The strength is genuine growth in the original business and access to international distribution; the weakness is the conversion of consolidated growth into cash available to the listed-company shareholder. At ₹984.50, market capitalisation is ₹1160.13 crore and corrected owner-earnings P/E is 31.36x, not the 25.13x obtained from total group PAT. A substantial Senores shareholding is valuable but is not operating cash or proof of a cheap stock. The unresolved EPS/minority reconciliations, poor group cash conversion and sensitivity to intra-period funding prevent a dependable entry valuation. The thesis is a **growth-plus-investment-holding special situation**, not a proven asset-light compounder. A cleaner legal-entity cash bridge and earnings reconciliation, followed by an adequately funded return above the hurdle, would change this conclusion. [AR26, AR25, RESIGN24, MASTER, TAPE]\\n\\n\"","\"Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\"","\"\\n- Exact issuer: **Remus Pharmaceuticals Limited**, CIN L24232GJ2015PLC084536, **ISIN INE0O5T01011**, NSE symbol REMUS. Do not confuse it with unrelated “Remus” pharmaceutical brands.\\n- Information cutoff: **20 October 2026, India time**. Market reference: **19 October 2026 official NSE closing price ₹984.50**. Source retrieval occurred later, but no later earnings, corporate action or market price enters the valuation.\\n- The active security is **SM / NSE EMERGE**. The September 2026 migration proposal received shareholder approval reported on 7 October, but that is not a completed main-board migration. Keep this research on the SME page. The exchange master supplies **11,784,000 issued shares** and **100-share lot**. Capitalisation is calculated with actual shares, not an unadjusted pre-bonus denominator.\\n- Scope: consolidated FY24–FY26 for the investable group; separately retained standalone FY20–FY26 for original-business history. FY24 is the later Ind AS comparative, not the unreconciled original Indian-GAAP vintage. Group five-year CAGR is **not available**.\\n- Horizon: five years from the reference entry, preceded by a projected 202-day audited-balance-sheet-to-entry bridge. Required return reference **15%**, with **12% and 18%** hurdles. ₹5 lakh liquidity reference is illustrative, not a stated user holding.\\n- **High confidence:** audited line-item transcriptions, exact security identity, corporate ownership chain and official daily trading observations. **Medium:** organic growth, underlying profitability and historical capital returns because of segment/perimeter changes and presentation issues. **Low:** current cash availability by legal entity, sustainable segment cash margins, current product-level approval register, financing commitments, and terminal realisability of investment assets.\\n\\n|Gate|Status|Reason and consequence|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|G1 Integrity / governance|Conditional, not Fail|No substantiated fraud or default finding; a real historical audit qualification, related-party acquisition and material RPT warrant scrutiny. Do not turn absence of an adverse finding into a clean bill of health.|\\n|G2 Accounting / evidence|Hold–unresolved|Reported EPS includes NCI earnings; subsidiary allocation tables do not reconcile cleanly; lease cash-flow classification and balance confirmations need clarification. Owner EPS can be corrected arithmetically, but segment economics cannot be repaired by guesswork.|\\n|G3 Funding / solvency|Conditional|Audited net assets are ample. However, demand borrowings, cash location and timing of collections matter. Optional refinancing, share issuance and investment sales are not committed liquidity. Model breaches describe assumptions, not an assertion of an actual company default.|\\n|G4 Investability / liquidity|Conditional|Active SME trading and lot size verified; spread, executable order book, current surveillance and binding circuit details not verified.|\\n\\n\"","\"Scorecard and Calculation Audit\"","\"\\nThe actual v3.0 framework, updated 3 October 2026, is applied without substituting an earlier framework. Its SHA-256 is 68d4de4255427df276318d0231530b867a453e740c74150e9768bf1387224137. All arithmetic uses Decimal precision 50. Display rounding is half-up; no intermediate rounding or missing-factor renormalisation.\\n\\n|Factor|Score|Confidence|Evidence IDs and limitation|\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n|GOV|5|Medium|AR23, AR24, AR26, RESIGN24, PLEDGE26: Current unmodified audits and controls, but historical qualifications, cash/earnings presentation, related parties and minority reconciliation reduce confidence.|\\n|MOAT|5|Medium|AR26, CALL26, FDA, OK-LICENSE: Country registrations, distributor relationships and RLD sourcing capability; no patent or owned FDA-plant moat, and the current jurisdictional licence register is incomplete.|\\n|REV|8|Medium|IPO, AR23, AR25, AR26, FY26: Standalone revenue grew from ₹11.919 crore in FY20 to ₹93.99 crore in FY26, with recent existing-scope group growth. This is not a spliced acquired-group five-year CAGR; acquisition and denominator limits prevent 9/10.|\\n|DUR|6|Medium|CALL25, CALL26, DECK26: Generic penetration, recurring comparator-drug needs and dossiers provide runway, conditional on funding, currency, launch and regulatory execution.|\\n|CAP|NS|Low|AR24, AR25, AR26: No reliable cohort or legal-entity incremental operating-capital bridge after acquisition and valuation changes. The reconciled broad proxy is reported but insufficient for attribution.|\\n|CASH|3|High|AR25, AR26: FY24–FY26 aggregate group CFO ₹26.49 crore / PAT ₹108.87 crore = 24.33%. Large receivables and inventory cause persistent weak conversion despite parent improvement.|\\n|BAL|6|Medium|AR26, TAPE: Large financial assets versus modest borrowings, but investments are not free cash; demand debt, FX, entity transfer and historical guarantee risks remain.|\\n|EXEC|5|Medium|CALL25, CALL26, AR26: Revenue execution is strong. B2C 14–15% trails the May 2025 management ambition of 25–30% and the 18–20% target recalled by May 2026 questioners. Registration cohorts are unverified and the clinical unit is pre-revenue.|\\n|EARN|5|Medium|AR25, AR26: Owner earnings grow, but forfeitures and OCI need stripping. EPS uses total PAT including NCI and subsidiary allocations conflict.|\\n|R|NS|Low|AR26, model assumptions: Cash forecasts are not current verified balances; legal-entity minority and free-cash claims remain unresolved. Conditional scenario returns are not underwritable expected returns.|\\n|D|NS|Low|AR26, model assumptions: Cash-breach scenarios cannot have ordinary drawdown valuations. Realisability and entity claims remain unresolved.|\\n|P|NS|Low|AR26, MASTER, TAPE, model assumptions: Observed owner multiples are corrected, but cash-funded intrinsic value and ownership/asset realisation are not sufficiently reliable for a price-versus-value score.|\\n|L|7|Medium|MASTER, TAPE: Framework starting score 9 at 1.971 theoretical days, explicitly reduced to 7 for 100-share lots, top-five-session concentration of 41.85% and the lower 20-session median. Median 5% capacity is two whole lots; unknown spreads/circuits are not adverse facts.|\\n\\n|Composite|Known weighted sum|Coverage|Exact lower–upper before cap|Final|Postcap display bounds|Confidence|\\n|---|---:|---:|---|---|---|---|\\n|BQS|4.74|88.00%|4.86–5.94|NS|4.9–5.9|Low, missing factors|\\n|GQS|5.45|85.00%|5.60–6.95|NS|5.0–5.0|Low, missing factors|\\n|IAS|0.70|10.00%|2.5650–8.6850|NS|2.6–8.7|Low, missing factors|\\n\\n**Exact contribution audit:** BQS = GOV 5×.20 + MOAT 5×.20 + REV 8×.12 + DUR 6×.10 + CAP NS×.12 + CASH 3×.10 + BAL 6×.08 + EXEC 5×.08. Known subtotal 4.74, coverage 88%, raw interval **4.86–5.94**, final **NS**. GQS = REV 8×.40 + DUR 6×.20 + EARN 5×.15 + CAP NS×.15 + CASH 3×.10. Known subtotal 5.45, coverage 85%, raw interval **5.60–6.95**. Persistent poor cash conversion with CASH=3 caps the interval at **5–5**, but **does not make the missing composite a numeric 5**. Final remains NS. IAS uses final BQS×.25, R×.35, D×.20, P×.10 and L×.10. Only L is available at the outer level, so coverage is 10%, known subtotal .70; nested BQS uncertainty gives exact interval **2.565–8.685**, final NS. Interval endpoints are bounds, not estimates or rankings.\\n\\nG1 is not Fail and GOV is not ≤3, so the integrity cap of 3 is not activated. G3 is not categorically Fail, so its IAS cap is not activated. No discretionary override fills CAP/R/D/P. CAP is missing because a broad capital bridge is not enough to identify incremental operating returns by acquired business. R/D/P remain missing because conditional scenario prices, especially after a funding breach, are not underwritable expected returns, downside protection or valuation support. Confidence in a displayed score is distinct from its numerical value. [AR23–AR26, CALL25–26; sections 8–10 below]\\n\\n\"","\"Business Overview\"","\"\\n**Two different businesses, plus a financial investment.** The parent develops dossiers, coordinates third-party manufacturing, registers and markets finished formulations in overseas markets. It does not earn a manufacturing moat merely by using approved contract manufacturers. Management described 35 approved manufacturers, about 20 active, with one related-group supplier and the remainder external, in the May 2026 call. Supplier substitution still requires quality, dossier and country-specific continuity. Product development, bioequivalence work, registration, pharmacovigilance, inventory, local distribution and collections remain economic obligations even without owning a large plant. [CALL26]\\n\\nThe US business sources comparator medicines used in research and bioequivalence work. This is pharmaceutical procurement and distribution, not ownership of innovator patents or a stream of patent-protected prescription royalties. FY26 RLD sales of **₹740.46 crore** represent approximately **86.7% of ₹853.63 crore group revenue**. Other disclosed revenue comprises formulations ₹93.48 crore, APIs ₹3.55 crore, retention/services ₹1.10 crore, export incentives ₹.81 crore and **advance forfeitures ₹14.23 crore**. Forfeitures must not be projected as repeat product demand. The model starts from recurring revenue of ₹839.40 crore, split into RLD ₹740.46 crore and other ₹98.94 crore. [AR26 note 30]\\n\\n**Who owns the economics:** Remus owns 56.67% of Espee Global Holdings LLC; the holding entity owns 90% of Espee Biopharma & Finechem LLC, giving **51.003% effective ownership**, not 100% and not a direct 51% shareholding. Relius entities in Bolivia and Guatemala are 99%-owned; the new Espee Clinical Trials business is 68%-owned. The clinical unit was incorporated in July 2025 and had no revenue and a small loss in FY26. The group statements consolidate 100% of subsidiary revenue before allocating profit to NCI. [AR26 AOC-1 and notes 1, 48; RESIGN24]\\n\\n**Geography and customer economics:** Group domestic/export classification was ₹325.26/₹528.37 crore in FY26. This is not a production-location breakdown. The parent alone recorded approximately ₹90.06 crore exports on ₹93.99 crore revenue. The two disclosed large customers contributed ₹132.50 crore and ₹67.13 crore. The latter amount matches related-party Rise sales, but the first customer is not identified here. No concentration-adjusted contract tenure or customer-retention cohort is available. [AR26 notes 30, 41, 45]\\n\\nManagement says RLD customers make 25–50% advances and stocks may be held until requested. That can improve funding, but only while advances, delivery acceptance and collections remain reliable. Customer advances actually rose from about ₹27.99 crore to ₹44.66 crore in FY26, while receivables and inventory rose substantially. Neither the verbal advance policy nor the reported advance liability removes the need to pay suppliers before receipt of final cash. [CALL26; AR26 notes 11, 13, 22, 24]\\n\\n\"","\"Moat / Industry Position\"","\"\\n**Mechanisms that may persist:** a reusable dossier and registration organisation, long-standing local distributors, knowledge of country approvals and a supplier network can reduce launch friction. Reliable sourcing of scarce comparator drugs can attract repeat research customers. These are capabilities, not automatically exclusive rights. The dossiers must convert into approved products, commercial launches, repeat orders and collected cash; revenue growth alone does not prove durable pricing power.\\n\\n**Registration ladder, not a TAM slide:** FY25 materials referred to 620+ registered products, 400+ commercialised products and 745+ dossiers. FY26 materials referred to 903+ approvals, 1,400+ under evaluation, 2,243+ dossier registrations, 850+ ready dossiers, 430+ commercialised products and 2,100+ SKUs. These are management categories with different denominators. They are not additive unique molecules and 430/903 is not a valid approval-to-sales cohort conversion rate. No regulator-verified product-by-country list with approval date, expiry, ownership, launch sales and contribution margin was obtained. Therefore registration counts support access capability, but not a high-confidence regulatory monopoly. [DECK26, CALL25, CALL26]\\n\\nManagement reported a cleared Peru DIGEMID audit and planned filings, Saudi tender/registration efforts and semaglutide-related filings. Audit, submission, tender eligibility, approval, commercial launch and collected revenue are separate milestones. This research assigns no unearned revenue to any of those milestones. FDA guidance explicitly distinguishes registration from approval, and wholesale-distribution authorisation requires appropriate state licensure and reporting. The one exact historical Oklahoma record located for Espee Biopharma & Finechem LLC is **closed**, ending September 2022, with no disciplinary entries shown. That is **not evidence that its current US operations are unlicensed**, nor is it evidence of current authorisation elsewhere. Current jurisdiction-by-jurisdiction licences remain an open diligence item. Unrelated similarly named UK and Peruvian entities were excluded. [FDA, FDA-LICENSE, OK-LICENSE]\\n\\n**Economic comparator set, not a peer-multiple shortcut:**\\n\\n|Comparator|Relevant mechanism|Why it is not interchangeable with Remus|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|Lincoln Pharmaceuticals|Export formulations and product registration|Own manufacturing means different capital intensity, quality-control responsibilities and margins.|\\n|Marksans Pharma|Regulated-market formulations, distribution and manufacturing integration|Owned overseas/Indian manufacturing and product exposure differ from RLD comparator procurement.|\\n|Caplin Point Laboratories|Latin American front-end distribution plus formulations|Owned manufacturing, clinical capabilities and US injectable activity differ from Remus's current segment mix.|\\n|Senores Pharmaceuticals|Regulated-market manufacturing and dossiers; Remus investee|A related financial investment, not an independent valuation benchmark. Using its market multiple for Remus operations would double count the stake and obscure different ownership claims.|\\n\\nOfficial company materials support the first three structural comparisons. No comparable listed Indian pure-play RLD distributor with reconciled segment returns was established, so no fabricated peer median P/E, ROIC or market share is used. The moat score stays 5: useful access and execution capabilities, exposed to supplier concentration, regulatory interruption, customer substitution and financing capacity. [PEER-LINCOLN, PEER-MARKSANS, PEER-CAPLIN]\\n\\n\"","\"Revenue Growth & Scale\"","\"\\n|Scope and interval|Start revenue ₹cr|End revenue ₹cr|Years|CAGR|Interpretation|\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\\n|Standalone FY20–FY26|11.9190|93.99|6|41.08%|Long-run original-business history, with accounting-basis transition; not the current group CAGR.|\\n|Standalone FY21–FY26|18.4181|93.99|5|38.54%|Useful five-year original-business scale signal, not a like-for-like consolidated cohort.|\\n|Standalone FY23–FY26|45.0273|93.99|3|27.80%|FY23 original basis and gratuity qualification disclosed.|\\n|Standalone FY24–FY26|63.94|93.99|2|21.24%|Later Ind AS comparatives; strongest recent parent comparison.|\\n|Consolidated FY24–FY26|212.94|853.63|2|100.22%|**Acquisition/full-year consolidation distortion. Not organic CAGR.**|\\n|Current consolidated group, five years|NS|NS|5|NS|No five-year same-scope history.|\\n\\nStandalone annual revenue, FY20–FY26, was ₹11.9190, 18.4181, 24.7171, 45.0273, 63.94, 79.16 and 93.99 crore. Annual additions were ₹6.4991, 6.2990, 20.3102, 18.9127, 15.22 and 14.83 crore respectively. The FY24 basis change means the ₹18.9127 crore bridge is not a pure volume/price bridge. Group additions were ₹407.42 crore in FY25 and ₹233.27 crore in FY26. [IPO, AR23–AR26]\\n\\nEspee's financial consolidation begins in FY24, with only a partial year; the definitive acquisition announcement was February 2024. It was a related-party cash acquisition, USD2.7 million, not internally generated growth. FY25 therefore captures a full-year scope step-up. By FY26, RLD revenue grew from ₹522.50 crore to ₹740.46 crore, about **41.7%**, while formulations grew from ₹69.37 crore to ₹93.48 crore, about **34.8%**. That supports existing-scope momentum, but a constant-currency price/volume/customer bridge is absent. The clinical startup had no FY26 sales; it is not the explanation for the growth. Do not classify all FY26 growth as rigorously proven organic growth. [AR24–AR26; RESIGN24]\\n\\nRecent half-years are **H1FY26 ₹400.20 crore and H2FY26 ₹453.43 crore**, versus H1FY25 ₹272.61 crore and H2FY25 ₹347.75 crore. Those halves sum to their annual totals. September 2026 earnings were not available by the cutoff, so there is no invented FY27 H1 or TTM roll-forward. Strong revenue performance coexists with a shrinking reported operating margin as low-margin RLD grows; scale is not a substitute for per-share cash generation. [FY26, H1FY26]\\n\\nREV=8 uses the documented original-business growth and recent existing-scope progress, with an explicit acquisition/history ceiling. It does **not** award a 100%-CAGR growth score to the bought revenue base.\\n\\n\"","\"Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\"","\"\\nThe practical scaling chain is: select product and country; secure compliant manufacturing and dossier rights; fund development and registration; win approval and distributor access; fund stock and receivables; collect cash; reinvest only the owner's share. Dossiers and SKUs are inputs to this process, not a measured addressable market. A bottom-up revenue forecast needs launches × sell-through × net realisation × retained customer share, plus repeat RLD demand. These unit cohorts are not disclosed, so scenario growth rates remain assumptions rather than an order-backed forecast.\\n\\n**Demand access versus delivery:** management targeted an increasing B2C contribution in FY25 commentary. The FY26 call reported only roughly 14–15% of non-RLD business, not of total consolidated revenue, versus a May 2025 management ambition of 25–30%; May 2026 questioners separately recalled an 18–20% target. Bolivia political/logistics delays were cited. FY26 added four named markets rather than automatically meeting an aspiration of five or more every year. Management cited 26 Bolivia launches and plans for another 40; these planned launches are not booked revenue. [CALL25, CALL26]\\n\\n**Capital is not negligible:** group receivables ₹139.84 crore plus inventory ₹72.06 crore are much larger than physical PPE. Clinical CWIP is ₹8.28 crore, with the new subsidiary financed by a parent loan around ₹9.58 crore. Continuing registrations, stock availability, export credit and supplier prepayments all consume capital. Acquiring a distribution platform can make accounting revenue rise quickly without establishing a high-return internally funded growth loop.\\n\\nThe broad invested-capital bridge in section 9 removes large financial investments and reconciles to operating capital, but there is no reliable segment/cohort bridge separating acquisition purchase price, goodwill, group transfers, operating advances, leased assets, currency translation and genuine incremental operating investment. Therefore **CAP=NS** despite apparently strong broad accounting returns. The model uses explicit growth-capital intensity, working-capital timing and minority claims; it does not reverse-engineer “asset light” into infinite growth at no cost.\\n\\nGQS remains NS, with the cash-conversion cap limiting any eventual numeric result to 5 on current evidence. To lift that cap, growth must produce sustained group cash conversion, not merely parent-only CFO or investment revaluation. Runway plausibly extends for several years, but duration is conditioned on regulatory continuity, collection performance and affordable capital, hence DUR=6.\\n\\n\"","\"Management, Governance & Execution\"","\"\\n**Audit history is not uniformly clean.** The IPO restatement report, printed F2–F3, expressly records qualified original FY20, FY21 and FY22 opinions because long-term employee benefits had been accounted for on a cash basis rather than accrued. Annexure 29 incorporates retrospective actuarial adjustments into the restated numbers. Printed F4 expressly distinguishes the restatement exercise from reissuing or redating the original opinions. Those old original audit reports were not separately obtained; the qualification history is supported firsthand by the signed restatement report, not inferred. FY23's audit was genuinely qualified on gratuity accounting: current-year expense of ₹10.22 lakh and an opening reserve adjustment of ₹19.51 lakh were at issue. The IPO restatement and later Ind AS statements are different vintages; their adjustments do not erase the original opinion. FY24's main opinion reads unmodified, while its basis paragraph refers to a qualified opinion and its other-matter paragraph acknowledges the prior modified opinion. This is an internal wording inconsistency, not a licence to label the whole history clean or to allege fraud. [AR23, AR24, IPO]\\n\\nParikh & Majmudar resigned effective 6 February 2024; Pankaj R. Shah and Associates succeeded them. The signed resignation letter and annexure state the reason as avoiding a potential future conflict of interest, with information-withholding concerns marked not applicable. The evidence does **not** support an allegation that the resignation resulted from obstruction or undisclosed wrongdoing. FY26's current audit opinion is unmodified, with revenue recognition a key audit matter; the standalone CARO and internal-financial-control reports do not report fraud/default or ineffective controls. These are positive assurance facts but do not guarantee recoverability of every balance. [RESIGN24; AR26 audit and annexures]\\n\\n**Assurance perimeter:** two small foreign subsidiaries were included using management-certified figures, approximately assets ₹11.80 crore, revenue ₹9.34 crore, PAT ₹3.06 crore and cash outflow ₹.15 crore. Two other subsidiaries were audited by other auditors, approximately revenue ₹755.79 crore and PAT ₹19.27 crore. The main consolidated report relies on those auditors for that work. Note 55 also states that confirmations of supplier, customer, loan and advance balances were awaited at approval, with book balances used. This is a material diligence limitation, not proof of fictitious balances. [AR26 consolidated audit, note 55]\\n\\n**Minority and earnings reconciliation:** main FY26 P&L total PAT ₹46.17 crore splits into owners ₹36.99 crore and NCI ₹9.18 crore. Reported EPS ₹39.19 corresponds to total PAT, whereas owner EPS on current shares is **₹31.39**. FY25 has the same issue: total PAT ₹38.42 crore versus owners ₹29.07 crore. Note 48 and AOC-1 show subsidiary profit/NCI figures that do not cleanly reconcile to the main allocation; for example, Espee profit ₹13.24 crore/NCI ₹6.49 crore differs from the main NCI and external-auditor perimeter. Do not calibrate a US segment margin from whichever inconsistent number gives the best valuation. Use main owner PAT for observed P/E and disclosed economic ownership only as an explicit forward proxy. [AR26 pp138–141, AOC-1, notes 48–49]\\n\\n**Related parties and allocation:** FY26 Rise sales ₹67.13 crore and purchases ₹10.19 crore sit within the separately disclosed ₹100 crore transaction ceiling; this report does not allege a cap breach. Rise receivables fell from ₹25.51 crore to ₹7.58 crore, which is a positive movement but not proof of collection of every outstanding related balance. Other items include Ratnatris purchases ₹8.35 crore, Senores development ₹2.14 crore, Renosen consultancy ₹7.37 crore, Espee Therapeutics ₹3.58 crore and Shashin Shah consultancy ₹4.41 crore. KMP remuneration and other benefits are separately disclosed. The operating rationale, pricing, recoverability and allocation to listed shareholders warrant continued review. Detailed related-party loan totals and presentation categories also need reconciliation rather than casual addition. [AR26 note 41; BALLOT25]\\n\\n**Capital allocation:** the Ratnatris stake exchange produced 3,261,744 Senores shares. The investment is carried through OCI, not operating PAT. A large rise in equity from investment marking is not evidence of cash reinvestment returns. IPO proceeds of ₹47.69 crore were reported fully used by March 2025, including working capital ₹30.31 crore, acquisitions/general purposes ₹16.38 crore and issue costs ₹1 crore. Reported utilisation is not proof that every resulting investment has met its hurdle. Historical guarantee/security exposure, about ₹75.35 crore in FY24, is not carried forward as a current obligation because later FY25 disclosures state nil and FY26 does not repeat it. However, exact current release/encumbrance evidence remains necessary before treating the listed stake as freely available cash. [AR24–AR26]\\n\\n|Dated statement or decision|Observed result by cutoff|Assessment|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|February 2024 Espee acquisition, USD2.7m, related-party|RLD became the dominant revenue stream; partial-year to full-year consolidation materially changed scale|Growth achieved, but compare owner cash and acquisition returns, not just sales.|\\n|May 2025 B2C ambition 25–30%; May 2026 questioners recalled 18–20%|FY26 call about 14–15% of non-RLD revenue|Below prior ambition; denominator must not become total group revenue.|\\n|Pipeline registrations and country expansion|Larger claimed pipeline; four named new markets in FY26; regulator-level conversion register unavailable|Partial operating progress, not all milestones independently verified.|\\n|FY26 clinical research expansion|CWIP and loans increased, startup remained pre-revenue|Capital deployed ahead of economic proof.|\\n|September 2026 main-board migration proposal|Shareholder approval reported 7 October; 19 October master still active SM|Approval is not completion and does not remove SME liquidity risk.|\\n\\nGOV=5 and EXEC=5 balance real growth and current assurance against these unresolved accounting, related-party and delivery issues. The May 2026 presentation reports March 31 ownership of promoters 71.0%, foreign portfolio investors 5.3%, alternative investment funds .8% and other public 22.9%. The April 2, 2026 promoter declaration reports nil encumbrance at March 31 and no encumbrance created in FY26; this is historical, not a current-date zero-pledge assurance. Latest promoter changes and insider transactions still require a cutoff-matched exchange update. The FY26 board report also records Anjali Shah’s CFO departure effective July 8, 2026 and Bhavik Shah’s appointment from July 9, approved June 23. No inference of wrongdoing is drawn from that transition. [AR26 board report; DECK26; PLEDGE26]\\n\\n\"","\"Financial Forensics\"","\"\\nAll figures below are ₹ crore; consolidated FY24 is the later comparable Ind AS vintage.\\n\\n|Year|Revenue|Owner PAT|Total PAT|CFO|Reported capex|FCF|Recurring EBIT|Broad IC|\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n|FY24|212.94|20.52|24.28|1.35|3.29|-1.94|26.07|39.43|\\n|FY25|620.36|29.07|38.42|6.98|3.98|3.00|34.73|62.00|\\n|FY26|853.63|36.99|46.17|18.16|14.57|3.59|39.71|93.21|\\n\\n**Parent-only history, kept separate from the acquired consolidated group:**\\n\\n|Standalone year / basis|Revenue|PAT|CFO|Reported capex|FCF proxy|Borrowings|Cash as reported|\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n|FY20 / Indian GAAP, IPO restated|11.9190|0.8116|1.3026|0.5592|0.7434|0.3593|1.1981|\\n|FY21 / Indian GAAP, IPO restated|18.4181|1.0258|2.8379|0.0664|2.7715|0.2789|0.8305|\\n|FY22 / Indian GAAP, IPO restated|24.7171|3.3857|5.0378|0.0710|4.9668|0.1917|1.1486|\\n|FY23 / Indian GAAP; gratuity-qualified original; not silently restated|45.0273|8.5028|5.3140|2.7316|2.5824|7.9500|3.0382|\\n|FY24 / Ind AS FY25 comparative restatement|63.9400|16.6200|6.3100|3.5900|2.7200|1.4400|1.7100|\\n|FY25 / Ind AS|79.1600|19.9000|15.5900|3.9500|11.6400|2.1400|3.4600|\\n|FY26 / Ind AS|93.9900|24.7100|25.5000|3.9200|21.5800|4.3300|2.7600|\\n\\nThe FY20–FY22 figures are IPO-restated observations, not evidence that the original audits were unqualified. FY23 is the original Indian-GAAP vintage, including cash/bank deposits in its reported cash definition; the later transition comparison separates approximately ₹1.01 crore cash equivalents and ₹2.03 crore other bank balances. It is not an unrestricted-cash series. FY24–FY26 use the stated Ind AS scope. Original and restated vintages remain separately identifiable in the inputs; none is spliced into a current-group five-year CAGR. Calculated trading gross profit and EBITDA, including recurring versions that remove forfeitures and non-operating income where appropriate, are retained in historical_metrics.json rather than represented as company-reported segment margins.\\n\\n**Revenue → earnings → cash.** FY26 PBT ₹59.16 crore plus finance ₹1.47 crore gives reported EBIT ₹60.63 crore. Remove other income ₹6.69 crore and advance forfeitures ₹14.23 crore to obtain **₹39.71 crore recurring EBIT**, approximately 4.73% of recurring revenue ₹839.40 crore. This is a research normalization, not a company-reported segment result. Owner PAT is not obtained simply by applying a generic 49% deduction to all earnings. The historical owner-core-PAT diagnostic removes 75% of forfeitures and other income from owner PAT, but it is deliberately not used as a definitive owner profit forecast because entity allocation is unresolved.\\n\\nFY26 cash flow starts with PBT ₹59.16 crore, reaches approximately ₹61.30 crore before working-capital movements after depreciation, interest, finance and other adjustments, and then absorbs approximately ₹31.81 crore into receivables and ₹20.50 crore into inventory. Payables contributed ₹4.76 crore and other liabilities ₹17.32 crore; other asset/liability movements complete reported cash generated ₹27.62 crore. Tax paid ₹9.46 crore leaves **CFO ₹18.16 crore**. Cash investing outflow was ₹15.03 crore and financing inflow ₹1.47 crore; net change ₹4.59 crore reconciles ₹7.23 crore opening to ₹11.82 crore closing cash. Component rounding is retained rather than forced into a fictitious adjustment. [AR26 cash-flow statement and notes]\\n\\nGroup CFO/PAT is approximately **24.33% over FY24–FY26**, with total CFO ₹26.49 crore against total PAT ₹108.87 crore. Aggregate reported capex was ₹21.84 crore and FCF ₹4.65 crore. Parent standalone CFO improved to ₹25.50 crore in FY26 against PAT ₹24.71 crore, but parent cash performance cannot replace group cash generation when most consolidated revenue and a large NCI claim sit elsewhere. CASH=3 reflects persistent group conversion weakness, not a claim that every subsidiary is cash-loss-making.\\n\\n**Lease presentation, preserved and normalized separately:** reported FY26 capex/cash purchase line ₹14.57 crore includes leasehold assets, while financing includes a positive lease-liability movement about ₹1.85 crore. Note 21 shows non-cash lease additions ₹2.57 crore, cash rental ₹.95 crore and interest ₹.24 crore. Removing the non-cash addition from both sides suggests cash capex around **₹12.00 crore** and lease principal around **₹.71 crore**, within rounding, rather than genuine cash funding of ₹1.85 crore. Reported FCF remains ₹3.59 crore; diagnostic capex-adjusted FCF is about ₹6.16 crore **before separately classifying lease principal**, not a second free-cash benefit. Gross ROU depreciation ₹.86 crore less ₹.19 crore capitalised explains net ₹.67 crore; gross total depreciation ₹2.94 crore versus P&L ₹2.76 crore is substantially explained by that capitalization and rounding. It is not left as an unexplained audit discrepancy. [AR26 notes 5, 21, 36]\\n\\n**Working capital and capital returns:** broad invested capital is defined as owner equity + NCI + borrowings + lease liabilities − cash − other bank balances − deposits − financial investments − loans. This gives approximately **₹39.43 crore, ₹62.00 crore and ₹93.21 crore** in FY24, FY25 and FY26. It includes goodwill and operating leased assets, and uses carrying values. Recurring EBIT ×75% is a standardized NOPAT proxy; dividing by average broad IC gives roughly 51% in FY25 and 38% in FY26. The incremental NOPAT/ΔIC proxy is much lower and volatile, roughly 29% then 12%. The operating side is net operating working capital plus PPE/intangibles, CWIP, intangible development, goodwill and ROU, plus other non-current operating assets: ₹53.25/₹89.33/₹135.63 crore. Financing minus operating capital is −₹13.82/−₹27.33/−₹42.42 crore, fully reconciled at disclosed rounding to non-deposit financial assets less non-advance current liabilities, provisions and current/deferred tax. The arithmetic residual is zero, but allocating deferred tax between financial investments and operations is not solved by that identity. Average operating ROIC proxies are 36.54%/26.48% for FY25/FY26, materially below the financial-asset-stripped broad ratios. Exact calculations, average owner ROE and total-capital ROCE are retained in historical_metrics.json. Those figures are **not verified segment/cohort returns**: acquisition scope, non-cash adjustments, investment stripping, OCI tax and customer-advance effects matter. They support further diligence, not a numeric CAP score.\\n\\nReceivable/revenue days at year-end are about 198, 64 and 60 for FY24–FY26; the FY24 denominator contains only a partial US year, so its apparent improvement is materially distorted. Inventory/revenue and payable/revenue days are disclosed as proxies only. Separately, reported purchases plus inventory-expense change gives trading COGS ₹163.37/₹532.87/₹742.21 crore; the calculation files include closing and average-inventory COGS days and average-payables/purchase days, with the partial-year FY24 limitation retained. FY26 receivables ₹139.84 crore plus stock ₹72.06 crore, less payables ₹76.04 crore and customer advances ₹44.66 crore, leave about **₹91.20 crore** before other operating balances. Even a modest collection delay can overwhelm cash ₹11.82 crore.\\n\\n**Liquidity, debt and assets:** FY26 borrowings ₹23.04 crore comprise ₹17.37 crore due within a year and ₹5.67 crore later; demand working-capital borrowing is approximately ₹17.04 crore. Leases total ₹4.03 crore, including ₹1.01 crore current. The maturity table divides borrowings ₹17.37/₹4.64/₹1.03 crore and leases ₹1.01/₹2.67/₹.35 crore across the disclosed buckets. Current deposits ₹24.50 crore are the current portion of financial-institution deposits with original maturity over 12 months, **not cash equivalents**. Other bank balances ₹1.06 crore, loans ₹9.65 crore, mutual funds ₹10.32 crore and AIF ₹7.21 crore are classified separately. Remaining disclosed capital commitments are about ₹.57 crore. No current credit rating or unused committed line was independently established. [AR26 notes 9, 12, 14, 20, 21, 42, 45]\\n\\n**Senores investment:** 3,261,744 shares cost about ₹20.55 crore and were marked at ₹257.03 crore at March 2026. Total FY26 financial investments were ₹274.56 crore. The October 19 official Senores close ₹1,253.60 implies **₹408.89 crore** for the disclosed share count, subject to no subsequent stake change. The holding quantity after March and current encumbrance are not proven by a price quote. FY26 OCI included a substantial fair-value gain; approximately ₹69.70 crore gross investment uplift and ₹60.15 crore total OCI explain much of equity growth without funding operations. The model neither treats OCI as EBIT nor adds both the stake value and capitalised investment earnings.\\n\\n**Per-share and foreign-currency discipline:** July 2025 1:1 and the earlier 3:1 bonus changed denominators, not economic ownership. Current issued shares are 11,784,000; authorised capital corresponds to 12,500,000 shares, leaving a modeled hard ceiling of 716,000 additional shares unless a future authorization occurs. No prospective authorization is assumed. Currency exposure notes disclose a net USD exposure equivalent to ₹15.91 crore and euro exposure around ₹.39 crore, but this is not a complete hedge or gross US-revenue exposure analysis. Translation, pricing currency, procurement currency and blocked/repatriable cash are different risks; the model stresses margins and available cash rather than assuming a fully natural hedge. [AR26 capital and risk notes; MASTER]\\n\\n\"","\"Valuation & Market Expectations\"","\"\\n**Observed entry metrics:** market cap ₹1160.13 crore; corrected owner EPS ₹31.39; owner P/E **31.36x**. The lower **25.13x** total-PAT multiple is not the shareholder's earnings multiple. Neither a group EV/EBITDA multiple that ignores NCI nor a P/E after subtracting the entire unencumbered stake at spot is used as a purchase rule. The financial holding and two operating engines require an explicit sum-of-parts bridge.\\n\\n### SME execution reality\\n\\nThe 60 official sessions from 24 July through 19 October show mean daily traded value ₹1,02,58,814 and median ₹50,73,552.50. The last 20 sessions show mean ₹94,15,796.50 and median ₹39,31,495, with no missing or zero-turnover observations. At the lower of the 60-day mean and median, a ₹5 lakh exit needs theoretical 4.9275 / 1.9710 / .9855 sessions at 2% / 5% / 10% participation. Whole-lot capacity uses each historical session's actual traded **shares**, not rupees divided by today's price: at least one 100-share lot fits on 34 / 57 / 60 of the 60 sessions respectively. At entry, one lot costs ₹98,450, five lots ₹4,92,250 and six ₹5,90,700. Fractions of a session, historical turnover and a quoted close do not guarantee a fill. No position-size or exit promise is inferred from this reference.\\n\\nThe framework starts at L=9 for the theoretical 1.971-day figure. The explicit judgment adjustment is **9→7**, reflecting observed concentration (the top five sessions supply 41.85% of 60-day turnover), the lower recent 20-day median and indivisible lots. Median 5%-participation capacity is two lots. Completed rolling exits take two sessions median for five lots and three for six, with maxima five/six and two right-censored end-windows. Unknown spreads/circuits are limitations, not observed adverse evidence. [TAPE, MASTER]\\n\\n### Cash-linked scenario construction\\n\\nThe executable inputs start with audited **31 March 2026 cash ₹11.82 crore**, borrowings ₹23.04 crore and disclosed working-capital balances. Seven pre-entry periods total **202 days** through October 19. Those are projected movements, not unaudited actual cash. No pre-entry refinancing, stock sale or issue is permitted; current issued shares must reconcile to the exchange master. The announced dividend is deducted in the pre-entry bridge. After entry, 60 monthly periods give five years, followed by 12 continuation months to test terminal operating feasibility.\\n\\nEach period records revenue, cash operating cost, target receivables/inventory/payables/advances, gradual balance transition, collections, supplier cash, tax, interest, cash leases, capex, principal and shares. Supplier cash is paid **before** receipts within each cash cycle; period-end cash alone is insufficient. The main case uses **8 cycles per month**, with 1/2/4/8/12/16/24/32 alternatives. This is an explicit timing assumption, not measured supplier credit. The frequency16 illustration is shown separately because changing cash timing materially changes feasibility; it does not replace or conceal the failed main case.\\n\\nOperating margins are before cash lease rent but after physical depreciation. Physical depreciation is .249% of revenue, based on approximate net non-ROU depreciation, and cash rent ₹.95 crore annually is deducted once. Tax is 25% of positive earnings after cash rent and interest, an assumption rather than entity-specific tax advice. Maintenance capex is .4% of sales; growth fixed investment is 3% of the **rolling prior-year revenue increment**; clinical completion spending is separately modeled. Working-capital balances move gradually toward scenario days/advance ratios. Demand debt is repaid in modeled post-entry month five, approximately March 2027, rather than silently rolled over, and other principal is amortised. New debt incurs interest beginning the following monthly period; higher-rate sensitivities expose that convention rather than treating it as free finance.\\n\\n|Assumption|Bear|Base|Bull|\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n|RLD annual growth|2%|12%|20%|\\n|Other recurring revenue growth|8%|18%|25%|\\n|RLD EBIT margin before cash rent|1.8%|2.5%|3.2%|\\n|Other EBIT margin before cash rent|18%|21.5%|27%|\\n|Receivable days target|85|60|50|\\n|Inventory / payable days target|45 / 30|34 / 34|28 / 36|\\n|Customer-advance / annual sales target|3.5%|5.3%|6.5%|\\n|Senores price growth / terminal haircut|−3% / 35%|6% / 25%|10% / 15%|\\n|Additional currency margin drag|.3 percentage points|0|0|\\n\\n**Financing modes are separate:** no-new-finance permits no refinancing, equity or discretionary asset monetisation. Deposit maturities are not treated as committed because exact dates are absent. Bounded-optional permits, only after entry, finite mutual-fund/deposit sales with a 1% realization haircut, then up to ₹15 crore incremental bank debt, then a **whole-share issue**, priced at 25% below entry with 3% issue cost. New issue count cannot exceed 716,000, authorised headroom, or ₹50 crore net cash raised. These are alternative possibilities, **not approved or committed funding**. Unspent investments are retained in terminal value; sold amounts cannot be counted again. No Senores sale funds a rescue. A dedicated stress removes liquid financial assets and new debt so that the whole-share mechanism is tested at zero, 100,000 and 716,000-share caps.\\n\\n**First breach is absorbing:** every pre-entry, intra-period, annual and terminal-proof cash event is tested against a ₹2 crore minimum. Once breached, ordinary CAGR and terminal equity return become **null/NS**. The engine retains the unpaid/failing path and shortfall but never manufactures a normal return on an unfunded operating plan. A terminal-proof failure also removes the ordinary return. A scenario breach is not proof that the real issuer ran out of cash; it demonstrates missing timing/funding evidence.\\n\\n|Scenario / finance|Status|First breach|Conditional five-year CAGR|Issued shares|\\n|---|---|---|---:|---:|\\n|bear/no_new_finance|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre1|NS|0|\\n|bear/bounded_optional|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre1|NS|0|\\n|base/no_new_finance|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre2|NS|0|\\n|base/bounded_optional|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre2|NS|0|\\n|bull/no_new_finance|CONDITIONAL|None|5.61%|0|\\n|bull/bounded_optional|CONDITIONAL|None|5.61%|0|\\n|bear/frequency16/no_new_finance|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre1|NS|0|\\n|bear/frequency16/bounded_optional|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|preentry/pre1|NS|0|\\n|base/frequency16/no_new_finance|CASH_BREACH_NO_ORDINARY_RETURN|investment/Y1M06|NS|0|\\n|base/frequency16/bounded_optional|CONDITIONAL|None|-4.57%|0|\\n|bull/frequency16/no_new_finance|CONDITIONAL|None|5.70%|0|\\n|bull/frequency16/bounded_optional|CONDITIONAL|None|5.70%|0|\\n\\n### Earned terminal valuation, not assumed rerating\\n\\nTerminal year-six revenue grows at 4%; operating discount/capitalisation rates are 14% RLD and 13% other, stressed separately. FCFF is derived from the **same 12 continuation rows** used for the cash test: after-cash-rent unlevered NOPAT + physical depreciation − actual continuation capex − actual change in operating working capital. Shared capital/cash flows are allocated by revenue explicitly because segment cash data are absent. No positive FCFF or no valid k−g means no supported terminal. A cash reserve equal to the continuation path's maximum cumulative draw plus minimum cash is retained; **only cash above that reserve** is added to equity. Debt is deducted, economic minority claims are applied to segment equity, and lease debt is not deducted again after rent has already been charged.\\n\\nRLD economic NCI is 48.997%, other-business NCI proxy 1%, both assumptions tied to the ownership chain and tested. The cash/debt allocation of 80% to RLD is also sensitivity-tested. This is not a claim that all subsidiaries can freely upstream cash. Add only the separately valued Senores holding after the stated haircut and 15% gain-tax reserve, plus unspent funds/deposits and explicitly impaired loan/AIF recoveries. The tax reserve is a conservative scenario assumption, not a determination of actual tax treatment. Future dividends are zero; all return measures are five-year price CAGR after modeled dilution.\\n\\n### Reverse expectations and sensitivities\\n\\nThe margin reverse test searches RLD EBIT margins **0–30%** with other base assumptions and frequency16 held fixed. Every scan and bisection trial is retained. It only bisects adjacent feasible sign-changing endpoints; any failed funding/terminal midpoint invalidates that bracket. No root is inferred across a cash-failure discontinuity. Bisection brackets and residuals are in reverse_results.json.\\n\\n|Target CAGR|Finance|RLD EBIT-margin root bracket|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|12.00%|no_new_finance|16.933938% to 16.933938%|\\n|12.00%|bounded_optional|16.933938% to 16.933938%|\\n|15.00%|no_new_finance|20.638201% to 20.638201%|\\n|15.00%|bounded_optional|20.638201% to 20.638201%|\\n|18.00%|no_new_finance|24.749833% to 24.749833%|\\n|18.00%|bounded_optional|24.749833% to 24.749833%|\\n\\nA separate revenue-growth scan covers **0–50% RLD growth** at fixed 2.5% RLD margin, without claiming continuity or an exact root across financing boundaries. The hurdle prices at 12%, 15% and 18% are computed exactly from ₹984.50×(1+hurdle)^5. These tests expose how much margin, growth and funding the entry price demands; they do not establish that the company can achieve it.\\n\\nOne-way tests cover opening available cash, collection days, inventory days, advances, cycle frequency, debt caps/rates, NCI, allocation of debt/cash, Senores growth/haircut, terminal growth and capitalisation rates, FX margin drag and rollover assumptions. Two-way tests cover RLD growth × margin and receivable days × terminal capitalisation rate. The whole-share rescue tests include insufficient-cap cases. All 312 unique paths and 502 aliases, including failed roots and duplicate evaluations, are preserved losslessly; paths.json.gz is a deduplicated path pool, not a summary that discards failures.\\n\\n**No supportable buy range is asserted.** A low model price in a failed path is not a bargain price, and a conditional positive return cannot overcome unresolved cash availability, minority and accounting gates. Any future entry range must be rebuilt with verified current cash, liabilities, contractual financing, ownership and a defensible capital allocation, then clear the hurdle without an unearned terminal multiple.\\n\\n\"","\"Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\"","\"\\nThe strongest bear case is not that revenue stops growing. It is that **thin-margin RLD growth consumes cash faster than suppliers and banks will fund it**, while the listed shareholder owns only about half of the US engine. Add delayed collection, slower inventory turnover, reduced customer advances and adverse currency economics. Cash fails before annual PAT reveals the problem; optional financing may be unavailable or dilutive; registration/clinical spending then competes with working capital. The model's early bear/base timing failures illustrate this mechanism without asserting an observed default.\\n\\nA second loss channel is investment concentration. A lower Senores price, a higher realization haircut, encumbrance or non-transferable ownership reduces the financial cushion. It is not appropriate to use a full spot stake value as immediate debt-repayment cash while also capitalising its future upside. A third is regulatory interruption at a CMO or distributor, causing approval delays, recalls, unusable inventory or loss of comparator supply. A fourth is poor related-party capital allocation that leaves listed shareholders with cash claims rather than distributable cash.\\n\\nPermanent impairment can arise from dilutive rescue, receivable write-offs, expired inventory, lost licences or an acquisition price/operating-cost structure that cannot earn its capital cost. A price drawdown alone is not permanent impairment; conversely, a large balance-sheet investment does not prevent operating liquidity failure. The **thesis-break variable is verified group cash generation available after minority claims and obligatory capital needs**, not a single revenue growth rate. No precise recovery value or downside score is fabricated for failed paths. D stays NS.\\n\\n\"","\"5× / 10× Feasibility\"","\"\\nA five-year **5×** price requires approximately **37.97% annual appreciation** and a price of **₹4,922.50**; **10×** requires approximately **58.49%** and **₹9,845.00**. At unchanged shares those imply market caps of about ₹5,800.67 crore and ₹11,601.35 crore. At a hypothetical 20× owner P/E, required owner PAT would be about ₹290.03 crore and ₹580.07 crore; at 30×, ₹193.36 crore and ₹386.71 crore. These are algebraic hurdles, not target multiples.\\n\\nAgainst FY26 owner PAT ₹36.99 crore, the required earnings growth is very substantial, before accounting for dilution, minority claims and capital funding. Group revenue growing rapidly at a 2–3% RLD operating margin does not automatically create those owner earnings. A multi-bagger case must demonstrate expansion of higher-margin formulations or durable improvement in RLD economics, repeatable cash conversion, sufficient capital and a supportable terminal value. The current dossier and commercialisation counts do not establish this. No 5×/10× probability is assigned, and no acquired-revenue CAGR is extrapolated to manufacture one.\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring Plan\"","\"\\n|KPI and baseline|Evidence needed / next milestone|Warning or thesis-break threshold|\\n|---|---|---|\\n|Owner EPS ₹31.39 versus reported ₹39.19 FY26|Company/auditor reconciliation of EPS numerator, bonus denominator, AOC-1 and Note48|Unresolved after next audited cycle, or further inconsistencies: keep G2 Hold.|\\n|Group CFO/PAT 24.3% over three years|Next two reporting periods and rolling three-year bridge; reconcile parent/subsidiary cash|Rolling conversion below50% while high growth persists is a warning; negative available cash without committed funding breaks investability.|\\n|Receivables ₹139.84cr and inventory ₹72.06cr|Ageing, subsequent receipts, inventory expiry and purchase obligations by entity|DSO >75 or material >180-day receivables without recoverability evidence; investigate rather than mechanically accept sales.|\\n|Advances ₹44.66cr; forfeiture income ₹14.23cr|Separate recurring sales from releases of historical advances|Profit growth dependent on forfeitures while customer advances decline undermines earnings quality.|\\n|Demand borrowing about ₹17.04cr|Renewal letters, covenants, unused committed lines and currency/entity restrictions|Renewal absent near maturity or inability to maintain a reasonable intraperiod cash buffer.|\\n|RLD ₹740.46cr; effective parent interest51.003%|Audited segment cash margin and shareholder-distribution reconciliation|Top-line growth without incremental owner cash; minority leakage larger than modeled.|\\n|903+ approvals /430+ commercialised, management categories|Product-country register, regulator status, launch dates and sales by cohort|Expiry, rejection or commercialisation slippage; no automatic credit for filing counts.|\\n|B2C about14–15% of non-RLD revenue|Like-for-like FY27 share, margins, cash costs and country milestones|Another miss against own targets without denominator-consistent explanation.|\\n|Senores3,261,744shares, Marchholding|Latest ownership/encumbrance and investment-allocation policy|Stake unavailable for holders, loss beyond haircut assumptions, or new related-party deployment without clear returns.|\\n|SMlot100; median60-session turnover₹50.74lakh|Updated official tape, spread/depth/circuit/series facts before action|Entry/exit not feasible within conservative participation; migration proposal alone does not cure it.|\\n\\nThese are research triggers, not a commitment to trade automatically. Update after the next financial release, material acquisition/financing, auditor clarification or listing-series change, using a new explicit cutoff.\\n\\n\"","\"Final Classification & Conditional Action\"","\"\\n**Classification: Hold–unresolved; not ranked.** BQS/GQS/IAS remain NS. This is not a “high-quality but expensive” label with a hidden numeric score; evidence gaps prevent that classification. It is also not a proven fraud, default or worthless business. The original business grew substantially, current audits contain meaningful assurance, and the investment stake is economically material. Those positives coexist with weak group conversion, minority-heavy distribution earnings and unresolved accounting/financing detail.\\n\\nThe compounder label is withheld because durable high incremental returns, self-funded growth, clean owner earnings and adequate entry return have not all been demonstrated. No buy/avoid trade instruction or portfolio weight is produced from the present conditional models. Price matters, but a lower quote alone cannot fix unclear cash claims. Reconsider after (1) reconciled EPS/subsidiary allocations, (2) verified legal-entity cash and contractual finance, (3) cash-converting product/customer cohorts, and (4) a cash-feasible owner return comfortably above the hurdle at a new price. Until then, the action is diligence/watchlist rather than ranking with fully underwritten companies.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\"","\"\\n### Source ledger\\n\\nSource IDs used above map to official documents and exact reviewed sections in source_register.json. Annual-report printed pages are used where available; the FY26 PDF contains facing-page spreads, so PDF page number and printed page differ. The working papers retain numerical transcriptions and public-source references, not a republished annual report.\\n\\n- **AR26:** [FY2025–26 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Annual-Report-FY-2025-2026.pdf), September 2026; standalone audit/CARO/IFC, consolidated opinion/other matters, full statements and notes, especially 5, 9–24, 30, 36, 41–45, 48–55. Main group statements printed 138–141; subsidiary and allocation tables separately checked.\\n- **AR25:** [FY2024–25 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Annual-Report-FY-2024-2025.pdf), September 2025; both audit reports/annexures, statements, restatement/transition, acquisition, investments, related parties, IPO use and guarantee disclosures.\\n- **AR24:** [FY2023–24 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual-Report-FY-2023-2024.pdf), August 2024; opinion wording, CARO/resignation, financials, acquisition and guarantee schedules. Original vintage retained separately rather than mixed into later comparatives.\\n- **AR23:** [FY2022–23 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/Annual-Report-2022-2023.pdf), August 2023; original qualified audit and gratuity treatment, statements and notes.\\n- **IPO:** [Final prospectus](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/Prospectus.pdf), May 2023; identity, capital, risk factors, business, objects, restatement report and financial annexures. Old history is not the present consolidated group.\\n- **FY26:** [May 2026 audited results](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/FINANCIAL-RESULTS-FOR-HALF-YEAR-AND-YEAR-ENDED-MARCH-31-2026.pdf), 20 May 2026; both audit opinions, annual and half-year comparisons.\\n- **H1FY26:** [September 2025 half-year results](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/UNAUDITED-FINANCIAL-RESULTS-FOR-HALF-YEAR-ENDED-30.09.2025.pdf), 10 November 2025; limited-review period only, not September 2026.\\n- **CALL25 / CALL26:** [FY25 call](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Investors-Conference-Call-Transcript-H2-FY25.pdf) and [FY26 call](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Investors-Conference-Call-Transcript-H2-FY26-FY26.pdf), May 2025/May 2026; management claims, targets and Q&A distinguished from audited facts.\\n- **DECK26:** [FY26 investor presentation](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Investor-Presentation-H2-FY26-FY26.pdf), May 2026; claimed registration/launch categories, not independent regulatory certifications.\\n- **RESIGN24:** [7 February 2024 board outcome and annexures](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/REMUS_07022024180724_Outcomeofboardmeetingsigned.pdf); acquisition price/related-party terms and signed auditor resignation reason.\\n- **MIGRATION26:** [7 October 2026 shareholder approval](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/REMUS_07102026191521_OutcomeofPostalBallotsigned.pdf); special resolution passed, not an exchange-effective migration.\\n- **BALLOT25 / BALLOT26:** [Rise transaction notice](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Notice-of-Postal-Ballot-10.11.2025.pdf) and [migration proposal](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Notice-of-Postal-Ballot-02.09.2026.pdf); ceilings/proposals do not prove completion or funding.\\n- **PLEDGE26:** [Promoter encumbrance declaration](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/team_bbodade_09042026155545_REMUS.pdf),2 April 2026, available 9 April 2026; nil atMarch 31 only.\\n- **MASTER / TAPE:** [NSE security master, 19 October 2026](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz) and [official daily bhavcopy](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/BhavCopy_NSE_CM_0_0_0_20261019_F_0000.csv.zip). market_extract.json contains all 60 exact-issuer observations, original URLs and archive/content hashes, including day counts; investment_market_extract.json separately records the Senores quote.\\n- **FDA / FDA-LICENSE:** [Registration versus approval](https://www.fda.gov/consumers/consumer-updates/it-really-fda-approved) and [wholesale-distributor licence checking](https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-supply-chain-integrity/check-licensure-wholesale-drug-distributors-and-third-party-logistics-providers), 2026 guidance available before cutoff. Used for terminology, not an assertion of issuer approval.\\n- **OK-LICENSE:** [Exact historical Oklahoma Espee record](https://lv.pharmacy.ok.gov/FacilityDetail.asp?CountyTypeLic=88-W-4787), update dated 11 September 2026; closed historical licence, not a nationwide conclusion.\\n- **PEER-LINCOLN / PEER-MARKSANS / PEER-CAPLIN:** [Lincoln FY25 report](https://www.lincolnpharma.com/Investor/Disclosures%20under%20Regulation%2046%20of%20the%20LODR/15.%20Annual%20Reports/2024-25/Annual%20Report%20F_2024-25%20LPL.pdf), [Marksans FY25 report](https://www.marksanspharma.com/pdf/annual-report/marksans-annual-report-2024-2025.pdf), [Caplin structural business description](https://www.caplinpoint.net/index.php/our-business/). Mechanism comparisons only; no subsequent financial result or spot peer multiple imported.\\n\\n### Reconstructibility and model limits\\n\\nThe authored inputs are historical_inputs.json, model_inputs.json, score_inputs.json, source_register.json, framework_provenance.json, market_extract.json, security_extract.json, investment_market_extract.json and this report template. Standard-library build.py/model_engine.py/path_codec.py regenerate all research tables, exact scores, liquidity, full cash-event paths, aliases, sensitivities and reverse trials. verify.py independently checks arithmetic, ledger identities, share caps, absorbing breaches, terminal cash/capital alignment and reconstruction. guarded_rebuild.py removes generated outputs and old attestations before rebuilding with an explicit read/write allowlist and denied network/subprocess calls. The compact recovery archive contains every minimal input and executable dependency; the full archive adds generated outputs. Neither requires the original source PDFs, OCR, a helper installation, network access or a private path to recreate the research calculations.\\n\\n**Material assumptions are not facts:** cash pooling; supplier-before-collection frequency; working-capital target adjustment; growth/margin/tax; useful life/maintenance/growth capex; debt maturity timing and uncommitted alternatives; issue price/fees/headroom; minority allocation; terminal capitalisation rates and continuity; available investment holdings, market growth, realization haircut/tax and residual loan recovery. The scenario engine makes these falsifiable, not known. Source-safe extracts reproduce the entered numbers and provenance, but cannot recreate the issuer's undisclosed contracts or prove future cash.\\n\\n**Open questions in priority order:** (1) corrected EPS and subsidiary allocation reconciliation; (2) current cash, restrictions, balances and bank renewal by entity; (3) customer/supplier confirmations and subsequent collections; (4) clean non-cash lease cash-flow reconciliation; (5) product-country regulatory register and commercialisation economics; (6) segment/cohort incremental capital and owner distributions; (7) current Senores quantity, encumbrance and capital-allocation policy; (8) recent shareholding/pledges and live execution constraints. These gaps are carried as NS or conditional status, not quietly converted into favourable assumptions.\\n\\nResearch update: initial v3 dossier with cutoff 20 October 2026. No publication or trade is implied. Changes to sources, inputs, scoring or models require a new deterministic rebuild and independent review.\\n\"","\"\\n### Source ledger\\n\\nSource IDs used above map to official documents and exact reviewed sections in source_register.json. Annual-report printed pages are used where available; the FY26 PDF contains facing-page spreads, so PDF page number and printed page differ. The working papers retain numerical transcriptions and public-source references, not a republished annual report.\\n\\n- **AR26:** [FY2025–26 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Annual-Report-FY-2025-2026.pdf), September 2026; standalone audit/CARO/IFC, consolidated opinion/other matters, full statements and notes, especially 5, 9–24, 30, 36, 41–45, 48–55. Main group statements printed 138–141; subsidiary and allocation tables separately checked.\\n- **AR25:** [FY2024–25 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Annual-Report-FY-2024-2025.pdf), September 2025; both audit reports/annexures, statements, restatement/transition, acquisition, investments, related parties, IPO use and guarantee disclosures.\\n- **AR24:** [FY2023–24 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual-Report-FY-2023-2024.pdf), August 2024; opinion wording, CARO/resignation, financials, acquisition and guarantee schedules. Original vintage retained separately rather than mixed into later comparatives.\\n- **AR23:** [FY2022–23 annual report](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/Annual-Report-2022-2023.pdf), August 2023; original qualified audit and gratuity treatment, statements and notes.\\n- **IPO:** [Final prospectus](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/Prospectus.pdf), May 2023; identity, capital, risk factors, business, objects, restatement report and financial annexures. Old history is not the present consolidated group.\\n- **FY26:** [May 2026 audited results](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/FINANCIAL-RESULTS-FOR-HALF-YEAR-AND-YEAR-ENDED-MARCH-31-2026.pdf), 20 May 2026; both audit opinions, annual and half-year comparisons.\\n- **H1FY26:** [September 2025 half-year results](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/UNAUDITED-FINANCIAL-RESULTS-FOR-HALF-YEAR-ENDED-30.09.2025.pdf), 10 November 2025; limited-review period only, not September 2026.\\n- **CALL25 / CALL26:** [FY25 call](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Investors-Conference-Call-Transcript-H2-FY25.pdf) and [FY26 call](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Investors-Conference-Call-Transcript-H2-FY26-FY26.pdf), May 2025/May 2026; management claims, targets and Q&A distinguished from audited facts.\\n- **DECK26:** [FY26 investor presentation](https://remuspharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Investor-Presentation-H2-FY26-FY26.pdf), May 2026; claimed registration/launch categories, not independent regulatory certifications.\\n- **RESIGN24:** [7 February 2024 board outcome and annexures](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/REMUS_07022024180724_Outcomeofboardmeetingsigned.pdf); acquisition price/related-party terms and signed auditor resignation reason.\\n- **MIGRATION26:** [7 October 2026 shareholder approval](https: